{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"pwd && rg --files -g 'AGENTS.md' -g 'PLAN.md' -g 'TASK.md' -g 'PROGRESS.md' -g 'package.json' -g 'justfile' -g 'Makefile' -g 'pyproject.toml' -g '\"'!node_modules'\"' -g '\"'!vendor'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"pwd && rg --files -g 'AGENTS.md' -g 'PLAN.md' -g 'TASK.md' -g 'PROGRESS.md' -g 'package.json' -g 'justfile' -g 'Makefile' -g 'pyproject.toml' -g '\"'!node_modules'\"' -g '\"'!vendor'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/gpt-6.1-sol-ultra.build-v2__20261106-045026__x07899e/work\nTASK.md\nPLAN.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat PLAN.md TASK.md; if [ -f PROGRESS.md ]; then cat PROGRESS.md; fi; cat /home/micha/.claude/skills/game-toolchain/SKILL.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat PLAN.md TASK.md; if [ -f PROGRESS.md ]; then cat PROGRESS.md; fi; cat /home/micha/.claude/skills/game-toolchain/SKILL.md'","aggregated_output":"Build an original, complete bomb-disposal puzzle game inspired by **Bomb Squad (1982, Intellivision)**. The player repairs expressive, hazardous electronics to uncover a hidden disarm code while a synthetic-voiced partner coaches and a theatrical saboteur needles them. Make precision satisfying, deduction consequential, mistakes recoverable, and the final code submission a small act of courage.\n\nThis is a production plan for **gpt-6.1-sol, reasoning effort ultra, with a 1.05M context window**. Use that capacity to keep the source research, engine contracts, game rules, content, interfaces, and verification consistent throughout the build. Deliver every required system as part of one polished vertical slice. Work to completion gates, without an arbitrary implementation time or token cap. Parallelize independent art, audio, content, and protocol review after agreeing on interfaces; keep integration and ownership explicit.\n\nYour own brief governs exact deliverable names, development-folder name, run metadata, and final artifact locations. All development paths proposed below are relative to the development folder you create. Choose the game's title yourself using the brief's procedure: silently produce 50 playful riffs on the source name, then select one with a real random-number generator. Carry the selected title consistently through the game and reports. Preserve the brief's exact output naming contract when exporting.\n\n1. Establish the source and technical foundation before implementation.\n\nRead the original [instruction manual](https://history.blueskyrangers.com/instructions/bombsquad.html), the [creator-maintained history](https://history.blueskyrangers.com/mattelelectronics/games/bombsquad.html), and the [original circuit screenshot](https://history.blueskyrangers.com/mattelelectronics/games/images/bombsquad/BombSquadScreen.gif). Keep a short source ledger in `docs/research.md`, separating verified facts, unknowns, and your original additions.\n\nThe verified source loop is timed repair followed by code deduction. Damaged display cells lead to circuits; successful repairs reveal evidence for one to three digits. The tools cut two terminals, remove a component, insert a jumper or replacement, and solder both terminals. A board's replacements follow a consistent shape-or-color rule. Frank gives work orders; Boris taunts. Skill settings change complexity and hazards. The visual identity includes a green circuit field, cyan traces, bright components, a tool cursor, and a conspicuous timer. Synthesized speech and warning sounds are established; an original music score is unconfirmed. Treat the campaign, new characters, and cooperation below as original extensions. Attribute the preceding facts to those primary sources.\n\nInspect the prescribed engine handoff and Hub integration directly, using their documentation and examples before inventing wrappers. References verified while planning:\n\n| Foundation | Verified reference | Implementation consequence |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Full handoff engine, version 0.89.1 | [README at inspected revision](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/blob/8ecc2f62353761187bdfe226f327b7ea5d2eb691/dist/handoff/README.md), [API card](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/blob/8ecc2f62353761187bdfe226f327b7ea5d2eb691/dist/handoff/API.md), [API index](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/blob/8ecc2f62353761187bdfe226f327b7ea5d2eb691/dist/handoff/API-INDEX.md), [assets guidance](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/blob/8ecc2f62353761187bdfe226f327b7ea5d2eb691/dist/handoff/docs/ASSETS.md) | Use the full engine, its native drawing and animation, input, shell, settings, and headless support. The agent edition omits asset-library rendering. |\n| AI Hub protocol 1, client 1.4.0 | [Protocol](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/blob/35a1047f2067b2af3de3943f0fe452f39105d869/lib/hub/README.md), [client](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/blob/35a1047f2067b2af3de3943f0fe452f39105d869/lib/hub/session.js), [harness](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/blob/35a1047f2067b2af3de3943f0fe452f39105d869/lib/hub/harness.html) | Cooperative play uses `shared-world`, host authority, automatic readiness, and the official lifecycle and harness. |\n| Standalone peer transport | [PeerJS getting started](https://peerjs.com/client/getting-started), [FAQ](https://peerjs.com/client/faq), [1.5.5 release](https://github.com/peers/peerjs/releases/tag/v1.5.5) | A separate, real standalone transport is needed; Hub's embedded client supplies no standalone hosting or signaling. |\n\nFetch the current prescribed handoff, record the actual revision used, and recheck differences against these inspected references. Follow its recommended reading sequence. Start from the complete kit whose shell is closest to a focused interaction game; inspect the point-and-click and adventure examples. The puzzle kit's falling-block rules do not define this game's mechanics. Read relevant implementation source when extending it. Put vendored dependencies and their licenses under `vendor/`; keep an explicit record of engine changes and reasons.\n\nBefore producing content, prove a tiny integration: engine scene, custom native component, animated tool, controller-selectable shell, procedural sound after user activation, and a deterministic headless state advance. Separately prove a Hub shared-world round and a standalone peer connection. These are development checkpoints; carry them into the actual game rather than delivering disconnected demonstrations.\n\n2. Commit to a focused player experience.\n\nUse **real-time puzzle / light simulation** as the genre, with a **fixed 2.5D diorama presentation and an almost overhead orthographic circuit workspace**. The city, service van, crew, and bomb housing give the work emotional scale. The PCB close-up fills the useful play area when precision matters. Camera transitions establish place, then settle into a stable working view. Let players inspect the city between operations without sacrificing an immediately readable timer.\n\nThe complete player loop is: receive a concise briefing, inspect the damaged code display, choose a useful evidence sector, claim its repair board, hear and read the work order, repair it, publish its clue cells, reconsider the code, and decide whether to repair more or submit. Successful submission disarms the device and resolves the city vignette. Time expiry or a deliberately confirmed wrong code produces a complete failure sequence and a useful retry path.\n\nKeep the player verbs concrete: select a sector, move or snap a tool, inspect, cut, grip, place, solder, extinguish, repeat instructions, return to evidence, and submit a code. The pressure comes from the fuse, repair order, imperfect information, heat, and your own decisions. Every supporting animation and effect should clarify one of these verbs or its consequences.\n\nUse new characters and dialogue: a reassuring but slightly frazzled technical partner, a smug gadget-loving saboteur, and a small municipal response crew. Give the partner succinct instructions and the antagonist occasional dry taunts. Keep the tone brisk, theatrical, and lightly comic. The antagonist operates through devices and radio interruptions. The game's dramatic climax is the disarm operation.\n\nThe main arc should take roughly **18–25 minutes for a first-time solo player**, including its short training sequence. This is a pacing target, not a build-effort limit. Cooperative cases can be shorter because players divide work. Tune each supported party size deliberately, and lock its workload and timer at case start.\n\n3. Specify the repair and deduction rules as real game systems.\n\nRepresent each repair as an explicit state machine. A targeted component starts attached at both terminals. Cut each terminal, grip and remove the freed part, place the required jumper or replacement in its socket, and solder both terminals. A completed component visibly becomes stable and tested. Complete the ordered list of required components to secure the board. Each input gives an immediate legal-target highlight, tool response, and concise result; finishing an animation alone never counts as a repair.\n\nWork orders identify components with position, silhouette, and an ID or pattern. Repeat the current order on demand at no penalty. Retain the latest meaningful instruction on screen until it is satisfied. A short order strip can show the next two tasks; opening the full order uses the same readable shell as other menus. Teach the full sequence with a friendly guided repair, then fade assistance as the player demonstrates it.\n\nReplacements obey one rule per board: matching shape or matching color identity. Give the player a consistent way to discover it through a test socket and example markings. Testing a spare consumes a small, visible amount of fuse time and reports continuity; it does not destroy stock. Players can test safely, infer from prior examples, or take a faster risk. A board's rule never changes after discovery. Ensure each offered correct replacement is unambiguous under that rule. Express color identity with a redundant pattern and label so every rule remains playable without color discrimination.\n\nThe tray includes enough spares and jumpers to complete every legal repair. A recoverable error must leave a repair route and a usable replacement. If a part is dropped, return it to a clear tray or salvage location. If a player leaves a board, persist the board exactly, including incomplete joints and heat. Returning to evidence never repairs damage or clears a hazard for free.\n\nDesign three understandable error paths. Cutting an incorrect terminal or violating the work order starts a clearly marked recovery window: restore the highlighted joint before a time/heat penalty applies. Installing a wrong replacement produces a failed continuity check and requires removal and replacement. Overworking the soldering iron raises visible heat; a fire requires the extinguisher before the board can be secured. Show what happened, the recovery action, and the time consequence. Use forgiving starting values—about an eight-second recovery window and brief solder holds—then expose and tune them. Avoid invisible instant losses during ordinary tool work.\n\nSoldering is a controlled hold with a visible safe completion band. Releasing in the band finishes the joint; excess holding adds heat. Focus snapping and a precision modifier help controller users. Touch assistance can finish a legal joint at its safe threshold. Extinguishing uses an obvious target and brief hold; its feedback must remain readable through foam and sparks. Cooling continues according to simulation time even when a board is not being viewed. An established fire stays active until extinguished or the case ends; cooling alone cannot erase it. Board selection and the persistent HUD show active fire and recovery deadlines, including on boards the player has left.\n\nFor deduction, use **one 4-by-5 binary stencil per digit**. Unknown cells, confirmed illuminated cells, and confirmed dark cells have distinct visual treatments. Dark evidence is just as meaningful as lit evidence. A successful repair reveals a small authored pack of cells in one digit rather than demanding a full circuit for every single cell. This preserves the source's two-scale relationship while supporting a tightly paced demo.\n\nDefine ten distinct digit glyphs and compute candidate compatibility against their actual cell data. Provide an always-accessible reference using those exact glyphs: the code-entry dial previews its selected digit in stencil form, and readable help lets players inspect all ten. The guaranteed evidence route must reduce every digit to exactly one candidate. An optional earlier route may do so with fewer boards. Permit risky code entry after two successful boards; the one-board guided practice explicitly bypasses this gate to teach confirmation. Clearly distinguish the act of selecting digits from the deliberate final submission. Additional boards offer certainty and time/score tradeoffs. Never require a blind guess to finish a case. Keep automatic candidate elimination as an optional assistance setting; the default view lets the player read the stencil and reason.\n\nCreate a solver and content validator that use the same glyphs and masks as the game. Verify useful evidence, eventual unique identification, reachable sockets, valid order references, recoverable errors, sufficient stock, and an exit from every nonterminal repair state. Reject authored or generated content that fails. Seeded variation changes codes, spare arrangements, and compatible board templates while preserving these invariants. Choose the code and all clue truth once at case start.\n\nScore successful disposal, remaining fuse time, clean work, and informed early submission. Explain the score with a few large result cards. Use score as a reason to replay; assistance and developer-modified runs are labeled in their results. Preserve a seed for retrying the same case and offer a separate fresh-seed replay. A wrong final code and an expired timer each resolve exactly once, with the cause stated plainly.\n\n4. Author a complete progression that demonstrates every mechanic.\n\nBuild three authored cases with distinct silhouettes, lighting, and radio exchanges. Use the following content targets as a starting layout, then tune them through full playthroughs. Board counts describe the solo version; a board can carry more than one ordered component repair.\n\n| Playable beat | Approximate pacing | Content and learning purpose | Required payoff |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| First dispatch / guided workbench | 2–3 minutes | One safe practice board teaches focus, both cuts, grip, placement, both solder joints, repeat cue, evidence, and code confirmation. Explain the fuse before live play. | Player performs a complete repair using ordinary controls and understands how a clue reveals a digit. Training can be replayed or skipped. |\n| Case one: a device at the waterfront | 4–5 minutes | One digit; roughly four evidence boards; short orders, jumpers, generous recovery, a coached recoverable mistake. Early inference should be possible. | First independent disposal; city lights return, crew celebrates, and score/retry/case-select are usable. |\n| Case two: the power relay | 5–7 minutes | Two digits; roughly six boards; introduce shape/color diagnosis, mixed spares, solder heat, and a controlled extinguisher lesson. Repeat the new mechanics across several boards. | The player makes a genuine choice about which digit or sector to investigate next and can recover from a wrong replacement. |\n| Case three: the central-city device | 6–8 minutes | Three digits; roughly nine boards; combine longer work orders, both replacement rules across different boards, heat, and recovery under stronger time pressure. Build its authored crescendo from learned mechanics. | A tense final submission, a proper ending, acknowledgments, results, and clean replay or return-to-menu flow. |\n\nEach case needs its own brief, work-order lines, antagonist beats, evidence layout, authored successful route, failure vignette, and recoverable-error opportunity. Ensure every core mechanic is available to each cooperative participant rather than hidden on one exclusive tutorial board. A quiet observation mode can show another player's ongoing repair without taking its claim.\n\nAfter finishing, case select and seeded replay keep the whole demo available. Include a short cooperative dispatch configuration drawn from the same cases for Hub rounds, using a duration that fits the advertised round limit. The full solo campaign remains complete. New cases should be additions to validated data and scene dressing, using the same tools and rules.\n\n5. Make the native presentation feel specifically about this source.\n\nUse the engine's crisp pixel-rendered 2.5D style as the visual foundation: substantial green PCB planes, pale cyan traces, chunky colored components, readable metallic tools, dramatic warm/cool work lights, and a layered waterfront/city backdrop. Preserve native coordinate conventions: x/y world plane, z up, and the engine's scale. Select an engine resolution/projection that keeps targets readable; render large text and interface labels through the native shell at display resolution. Validate the combination early at 1920-by-1080.\n\nBuild original component silhouettes, both-terminal socket variants, spare tray, cutter, pliers, soldering iron, extinguisher, test socket, fuse display, stencil display, bomb housings, response-van dressing, and the three case landmarks. Generic furniture, street props, and background details can use the asset library. Keep trace decoration distinct from interactable terminals. A target should retain its silhouette, state pattern, and highlight in grayscale.\n\nUse native asset definitions and rendering: extend `E.SHAPES`, `E.MATERIALS`, and `E.addAssets` as documented, render through the engine renderer, and use its pixel primitives for custom surfaces. Keep the resulting assets consistent with native lighting and projections. Prefer the full native engine path over constructing a second renderer for the game.\n\nExtend native animation with cutter compression, separated leads, plier grip and lift, part insertion, solder flow, cooling joints, diagnostic pulses, foam, sparks, case opening, countdown escalation, and the disarm release. Build crew portraits and physical reactions with the native Humanoid rig. Relevant extension points include `E.Humanoid.pose`, tool/attachment hooks, `rig.update`, `game.tween`, and `game.script`; verify exact signatures in the revision used. Animation presents authoritative state and can be interrupted safely by pause, scene transition, disconnect, or retry.\n\nGive every consequential action a distinct anticipation, contact, and settle response. Completed boards should visibly quiet down. Newly published evidence should travel or flash into the global display without hiding it. On success, replace the oppressive ticking with a brief release and a city response. On failure, show a stylized skyline consequence and a clear retry decision. Reduced motion softens camera travel, shaking, and flashes while retaining all information.\n\nKeep the active work area generous. The persistent HUD needs the fuse, current task, selected tool, compact shared evidence access, and a small context prompt. Put detailed instructions, settings, and feedback on separate controller-readable pages. Reserve visual complexity for the world and tool feedback; maintain a calm hierarchy around the next actionable terminal.\n\n6. Synthesize the entire soundscape in code.\n\nUse the engine's procedural audio as the base, extending Web Audio synthesis where necessary. Create cutter snaps, plier clicks, insertion sounds, solder hiss, continuity tones, foam bursts, a fuse pulse, distant sirens, radio chirps, city ambience, failure punctuation, and a disarm flourish. Do not ship recorded, embedded, downloaded, or generated audio files.\n\nCompose an original restrained electronic cue system: an unobtrusive investigation pattern, a more urgent layer as the fuse falls, and short case/result phrases. Set **music to 10% by default** in the actual settings and gain graph. Provide independent music, effects, and voice controls, including mute. Read back and test the real default rather than relying on a label.\n\nGive the partner and saboteur different code-generated gibberish voices using pitch, envelope, rhythm, and formant-like filtering. Their readable caption text carries every instruction, warning, and joke. Prioritize urgent repair instructions over taunts; a taunt must never replace an unfinished work order. Repeat replays the current instruction caption and its gibberish cue. Debounce and queue barks so reconnects or snapshot updates do not retrigger them.\n\nInitialize or resume audio through supported user activation. An unattended attract sequence can remain silent until activation; its visual captions must still explain it. Stop or suspend appropriate audio when the game pauses, loses its session, reaches Hub `end`, or is disposed. Validate synthesis with `OfflineAudioContext` or an analyser: useful nonzero output, finite values, envelopes, bus gains, and no uncontrolled clipping. Report listening quality as unverified on this machine.\n\n7. Design one readable interface for all input methods.\n\nExtend the existing engine pause menu, settings, rebindings, fullscreen behavior, shell, and menu focus. `E.Shell` offers TV sizing; `E.Menu` handles controller navigation and pointer-hover focus. Use the documented APIs before replacing them. Start with PlayStation glyphs and expose the engine's Xbox A/B/X/Y option. Generate prompts through the input label system so changing device or rebinding updates the same screens.\n\n| Action | Standard-mapped controller default | Keyboard and mouse default | Touch behavior |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Move between targets | Left stick or D-pad, directional snap | WASD/arrows for focus; mouse hover selects the actual target | Direct tap; invisible drag region for fine tool motion when useful |\n| Apply selected tool / confirm | Cross; hold when required | Left click or Space/Enter | Contextual pop action near the selected target; short assisted holds |\n| Back / drop safely | Circle | Escape in overlays; right click for drop/back in workspace | Tap the contextual back/drop affordance |\n| Cycle tools | L1/R1; hold a shoulder for a readable tool wheel if useful | Mouse wheel; 1–4 for the four physical tools | Contextual wheel or automatic legal-tool selection |\n| Precision / inspect | L2 for precision; Square for test/inspect | Shift for precision; E or middle click for inspect | Target snapping, enlarged hit regions, automatic legal-joint assistance |\n| Repeat work order | Triangle | R | Tap the current instruction |\n| Evidence / board view | R2, with Touchpad as an optional shortcut | Tab; clickable evidence display | Tap the compact evidence display |\n| Pause / menu | Options, respecting Hub's Create+Options chord | Escape | One persistent menu button |\n\nCheck bindings against the engine and actual gameplay, resolve any conflicts, and teach them in context. Left-clicking a target must operate on that target, not the previously focused one. Keep focus changes separate from destructive actions. Tool switching never discards a held part silently. Avoid requiring rapid cursor precision while the camera is moving.\n\nBuild a coherent screen flow: title, training/case select, short brief, play, pause, settings, developer tools, results, ending, About/Credits, and standalone host/join. Use the same menu components and navigation rules throughout. Settings include audio, glyph family, remapping, motion/flashes, readable text scale, and puzzle/tool assistance. Restore focus to a valid element when overlays close. Handle controller disconnect with an intelligible prompt and the correct pause policy.\n\nUse large text, generous targets, visible focus, safe screen margins, and concise instructions. Judge the 10-foot composition at 1080p. Retain the same game and screens for keyboard/mouse. On phones and tablets, use responsive scaling and a larger active workspace, a menu button, direct target selection, multitouch, invisible motion controls where needed, and contextual actions. Avoid a permanent wall of virtual controller buttons. Rotate/reflow the workspace sensibly; no physical-touch or real-iOS claim can come from browser emulation alone.\n\n8. Implement cooperative multiplayer as a complete shared game.\n\nSupport **one to four players**, with one shared bomb, code, evidence ledger, city state, and deadline. Each player claims a repair board and works its tool sequence on their own view. Everyone sees automatic board-completion and evidence updates. This supports useful concurrent work while retaining a collective disposal decision. Case definitions provide sufficient meaningful boards for all active seats. Scale work orders and the fuse openly for party size at start; joining or leaving does not secretly accelerate a running clock.\n\nUse one authoritative simulation in solo, Hub, and standalone co-op. Transport adapters carry the same semantic commands and state updates. A board claim is atomic and exclusive. Consequential commands include case ID, board ID, target ID, action ID, expected revision, and tool operation. Take sender identity from the transport, validate ownership and legal state, and make duplicate delivery idempotent. Remote cameras, focus, and cursor motion remain local presentation. Replicate accepted tool operations and resulting board state; physics synchronization is unnecessary.\n\nRepresent holds as validated begin/end actions with a host-bounded duration; a dropped connection cannot leave a soldering tool running forever. Release all holds when input focus or control is lost. Preserve an append-only set of truthful evidence cells. Emit cues from accepted event IDs so resync cannot replay every sound or celebration. Use labeled seeded random streams for case generation and cosmetic variation, with cosmetics unable to change puzzle state.\n\nThe host owns deadlines, heat, repairs, score, and terminal outcomes. Clients display time derived from the authority and request a snapshot before accepting actions after reconnect. Rendering and frame rate never determine fuse duration. Solo pause freezes the simulation. Online menus are local, visibly say the fuse continues, and do not pause authority. Configure the documented online `live:true` / `autoPause:false` behavior and nonpausing menu path as appropriate; native blur, hidden-tab, and controller-loss auto-pause must not stall the host independently of the menu. In online play, preserve elapsed time under background-tab throttling; follow Hub's explicit hidden-host termination rules. Define boundary ordering so accepted actions and expiry cannot produce both win and loss.\n\nFor shared code entry, a player proposes the code and intentionally confirms submission; this counts as their yes vote. Connected seated players can accept or veto during a five-second window. Any veto cancels the proposal. Unanimous acceptance submits immediately; otherwise the deadline submits only with a strict majority of eligible seats accepting and no veto. Without that majority, cancel and resume work. Remove departed seats from eligibility, exclude new seats until the next proposal, and cancel if the proposer leaves. A solo player uses deliberate confirmation without a vote. The host resolves the proposal once, and the fuse continues throughout. These are game actions, not a chat system.\n\nOn guest disconnect, cancel held actions and release their board according to the adapter's explicit connection state. A standalone reconnect within a brief grace period can reclaim its seat using a room-scoped token, after a complete state sync. Reject stale commands from the old connection. A Hub `leave` is final for that round and is not a reconnect opportunity. Guest departure must not strand a required component or lock code entry. Host departure ends the online round clearly; standalone players can return to menu and start a fresh case. Follow Hub's void/end rules inside Hub. Host migration is unnecessary for this slice.\n\nInside AI Hub, paste its **unmodified client as the first script in the head**, immediately after the charset declaration. Add the required session metadata for protocol 1, shared-world, one-to-four seats, goal, controls, and appropriate round duration. Connect through `AIHub.connect({mode:'shared-world', seats:[1,4]})`, attach lifecycle handlers, and call `ready()` once automatically when loaded. Use the Hub's standard card. Active sessions enter the round without title, training, attract mode, room entry, or other gates. Begin simulation only after `start` and when `AIHub.time()` reaches zero.\n\nOnly the host simulates, including when `AIHub.server` is true and the host has **no seated player**. Treat host authority and local player as separate concepts. Use `AIHub.rng(label)` for shared-world generation. Carry intents and state through `AIHub.send` and `message`; the Hub permits JSON packets up to 16 KB and 20 outgoing messages per second, with a burst allowance. Keep discrete operations reliable, coalesce updates, and send compact snapshots or board chunks with explicit revisions. Respect these protocol limits without turning the project into an optimization exercise.\n\nFinish the team round once through host `finish({standings, scores, won})`, tying the remaining cooperative seats. Handle `time-up` within its grace period. On `end`, stop gameplay input, sound, and outbound traffic. Respect Hub-supplied names as text, its storage isolation, fullscreen delegation, and Create+Options ownership. Use only Hub networking in an active Hub session. Keep custom chat, voice communication, emotes, custom names, browser dialogs, and navigation out of that session flow.\n\nOutside Hub, `connect()` returning null selects the ordinary game. For standalone online play, implement a **PeerJS 1.5.5 adapter** using secure cloud signaling and WebRTC data connections. Host creates a namespaced, randomly generated room ID; guests enter its short displayed code. Use controller-operable character selection alongside keyboard entry/paste. Validate protocol/content versions before admitting a seat, show connection and ready states, and support full/rejected/unavailable rooms, timeouts, retry, back, disconnect, and cleanup. Host and join must produce an actual peer connection and a playable co-op case.\n\nInstantiate PeerJS only in standalone online flow. Its official guide documents `new Peer`, `peer.connect`, the incoming `connection` event, and the connection `open`/`data`/`close` lifecycle; confirm options against the pinned release. Destroy owned peers on exit. PeerJS Cloud supplies signaling, but cross-network connectivity can require TURN. Ship actionable retry/back behavior and record the tested network boundary. Do not invent credentials, assume access to Hub's authenticated TURN service, or describe two tabs alone as proof of universal internet connectivity. A local self-hosted signaling fixture may aid deterministic tests; verify the shipped signaling path as well.\n\n9. Build content and developer tools that make the next cases ordinary authoring work.\n\nUse a modular source tree with clear ownership. Suggested layout:\n\n| Development path | Responsibility |\n| --- | --- |\n| `src/main.js`, `src/game/` | Startup, scene flow, save migration, campaign and session lifecycle |\n| `src/sim/` | Pure repair rules, clocks, heat, code/evidence logic, scoring, command validation, deterministic state |\n| `src/content/` | Case definitions, board templates, glyphs, authored clue masks, dialogue, default tuning |\n| `src/view/`, `src/art/` | Native engine scenes, original assets, tool/crew animations, camera and feedback |\n| `src/ui/`, `src/input/` | Shell screens, focus, prompts, input actions, touch assistance |\n| `src/audio/` | Procedural instruments, cue scheduler, voice synthesis, bus settings |\n| `src/net/` | Session abstraction, Hub adapter, standalone peer adapter, state codec |\n| `src/dev/` | Live tuning, board/case editor, validation, inspection, test controls |\n| `tests/`, `tools/` | Simulation tests, real-input playthrough driver, audio checks, Hub fixtures, bundling and verification |\n| `docs/`, `evidence/` | Research, controls, authoring notes, requirement coverage, observed issues and test evidence |\n\nKeep the simulation independent of the renderer, browser input, audio, and transport. Presentation consumes state and events; it never mutates the puzzle during drawing. Use the engine's fixed-step facilities and a clearly defined clock policy rather than creating competing update loops. Verify disposal and scene-transition behavior, including the engine's timer-clearing rules.\n\nA case definition should contain its ID, brief, theme, digit count, glyph/code source, evidence sectors, repair-board references, time/workload per party size, dialogue cues, introduced mechanics, and ending beats. A board template should contain component positions and terminal anchors, required job order, replacement rule, spare tray, hazard settings, and evidence reward. IDs remain stable across rendering, saves, networking, tests, and editor exports. Content variation is seeded and versioned.\n\nExpose developer mode through pause. Provide case/seed selection, restart/checkpoint, single-step and time scale for solo testing, fuse adjustment, component/state inspection, fault/fire injection, reveal/solve actions, input display, and network status. Clearly mark modified runs and isolate them from ordinary records. Hub sessions retain local inspection and presentation settings; gameplay-changing cheats, tuning, and editor controls are disabled in live session play. Standalone cooperative test tuning requires host authority and broadcasts its revision to all peers.\n\nUse native settings sliders with live application and reset-to-defaults. Expose fuse duration/time scale, tool travel/precision assistance, solder safe-band/heat gain/cooling, recovery-window length, penalty size, fire intensity/extinguish rate, and hint delay. Define what happens to an in-progress action when its setting changes. Keep ranges finite and safe; resetting restores authored defaults coherently. Relevant native APIs include `settings.define` with slider type and an `apply` function, and `settings.reset(group)`.\n\nProvide a small practical board/case authoring panel in dev mode: load a template, place/select components and terminal anchors, order required repairs, choose rule and spares, paint evidence masks on the digit stencil, set case pacing, validate, and playtest a scratch copy. Support versioned JSON import/export with explicit save feedback. Keep game records and original templates safe from scratch editing. Include a short authoring guide and demonstrate adding a small valid board through this workflow. This panel serves the narrow content model; it does not need to become a general-purpose level editor.\n\nVersion local settings and progress saves. Save case unlocks, best results, assistance state, and replay seeds at safe boundaries. Treat unavailable or corrupted storage as recoverable and continue play with defaults. Hub's isolated storage must not contaminate ordinary progress. Keep an issue ledger as testing proceeds so final feedback can cite actual discovered defects and engine changes.\n\n10. Make attract mode a convincing, isolated demonstration.\n\nAfter **10 seconds without input on the standalone title/menu**, start a looping AI-driven sequence that uses the actual repair simulation and presentation. Use the native `E.Arcade` idle/attract support where it fits; verify its exact lifecycle. Show a complete cut/grip/place/solder repair, a shape/color test, a recoverable fire, evidence discovery, a code submission, and the city payoff across a short rotating playlist. The attract actor may cheat as permitted, but its actions must visibly demonstrate the mechanics.\n\nAttract mode owns a separate local state, seed, records context, and audio lifecycle. It never joins a room, calls session readiness, writes progress, or changes settings. Display a large invitation and a clear demo indicator. Any controller, keyboard, mouse, or touch input exits immediately to a stable menu; consume that wake input so it does not also submit a choice or tool action. Reset idle tracking on genuine input, not noisy gamepad axes. Do not interrupt text entry, active online lobbies, developer editing, or active Hub cards with attract play.\n\n11. Implement through integrated completion gates.\n\nUse these gates in order, with presentation, controls, and testing accompanying the relevant system. Each gate produces working game behavior; required features remain part of the final delivery.\n\n| Gate | Work to complete | Evidence needed to proceed |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Source and contracts | Research ledger, title procedure, engine/Hub versions, chosen projection, input actions, state/command/content schemas, miniature engine and transport proofs | Verified primary references; authentic native rendering/animation; a real standalone peer link; a Hub authority without a local player |\n| Complete repair loop | One attractive board, all physical tools, correct order, full repair state machine, diagnosis, recovery, heat/fire, initial evidence and code submission | Real controller and mouse paths can complete and fail the board; state tests cover legal and illegal transitions |\n| First finished case | Briefing, readable HUD, original scene/assets/animations, procedural cues/voices, pause/settings, win/loss/retry/results | A complete normal-input case, including deliberate recoverable error and early deduction; 10-foot visual review |\n| Shared play | Same simulation through Hub and standalone adapters, ownership, sync, confirmation, disconnect lifecycle, party scaling | Two real peers dispose a device; Hub harness scenarios pass; authority-only server works; no leaked non-Hub networking |\n| Full authored slice | Training, all three cases, campaign ending, case select/replay, every mechanic's taught and combined use, saves | Whole-campaign scripted playthrough using ordinary commands; all content validates; no missing progression or scene |\n| Production tools and presentation | Dev tools/sliders/reset, editor/import/export, attract playlist, touch adaptation, complete original audiovisual feedback, About/Credits | Editor-authored content round-trips; attract wakes cleanly; every input reaches every required screen |\n| Delivery | Single-HTML bundle, standalone and Hub smoke checks against that artifact, final feedback/report data, credits and licenses | Final acceptance matrix passes; report parses and matches actual evidence; output names and locations match the builder's own brief |\n\nIf an engine abstraction gets in the way, modify it with a recorded reason and a focused regression check. Keep the changes small enough to understand in the context of the whole game. Use the large context to audit cross-system assumptions before they become late integration failures: pause versus host time, animations versus authoritative repairs, masks versus glyphs, tools versus touch, packaging versus client injection, and feedback data versus actual test records.\n\n12. Verify behavior, then verify the final artifact.\n\nPut all automated checks behind **`node tools/verify.mjs`**. It should print only failures and one final count line, exit nonzero on failure, and support focused groups for changed areas. Record results in machine-readable evidence files while keeping console output quiet. Rerun a group when its covered code or content changes. Run the aggregate after the final relevant changes. Wrap any noisy native tools rather than losing their error information.\n\nUse the engine's documented headless tools, including its headless game mode, deterministic advance, input feeding, snapshots, error collection, and disposal. Its handoff also supplies checking, replay, filmstrip, and asset-sheet tools. Confirm signatures from the version used. Use real browser tests for input, shell, rendering lifecycle, transport, audio activation, and bundling. Tests should establish behavior and invariants rather than duplicate implementation logic.\n\n| Verification area | Required checks |\n| --- | --- |\n| Repair model | Every valid state transition; both terminals required; wrong order/cut/part recovery; dropped parts; safe solder band; overheating/fire/extinguishing; leaving and revisiting a partial board; duplicate commands; no stranded stock or stuck holds |\n| Code and content | Distinct actual glyphs; lit/dark/unknown evidence; consistent reveal packs; proof of unique guaranteed solution for authored cases and generated variants; useful early inference; deliberate wrong submission; all IDs and orders valid |\n| Time and outcomes | Single terminal outcome at deadline boundaries; solo pause/resume; online menus; time scale/reset; background/resume; fire and heat while away from a board; retry seed fidelity |\n| Full playthrough | Ordinary controls from fresh start through training, all cases, ending, results, case replay, and credits; exercise every mechanic; include recovery and separate time-expiry/wrong-code failure routes; no direct state writes or invulnerability in the acceptance run |\n| Controller | Standard-mapped gamepad stub via browser initialization; focus, repeat, tool changes, precision, submission, pause, dev sliders, glyph switching, disconnect/reconnect, and no accidental wake action |\n| Keyboard/mouse | Hover moves focus; click acts on hovered target; keyboard-only menu reachability; expected keys; wheel/tools; rebinding labels; overlays restore focus; input-device changes update prompts |\n| Touch | Phone/tablet profiles, taps, drags, multitouch, contextual actions, assisted solder, orientation/reflow, and reachable menu/settings; clearly record these as emulated checks |\n| Audio | User activation, correct default music gain of 0.1, mute/bus controls, finite nonzero synthesis, voice priority, caption completeness, no repeated snapshot barks, no continuing sound after Hub end |\n| Attract | Starts after ten idle seconds; loops the full playlist; any input exits; wake event consumed; no save/score/settings changes; no network side effects; no active-play interruption |\n| Standalone network | Actual shipped host/join path in two pages; version mismatch/full/unavailable room; several repairs by different seats; canonical-state agreement; shared code result; disconnect and snapshot rejoin; owned-peer cleanup; failed signaling/NAT state is actionable |\n| Hub network | Official automatic checks, normal two-player round, documented four-player latency/jitter/loss case, host-leave, time-up/end, and `scenario=server`; assert `window.__hubCheck.pass === true` and the harness completion marker, then also assert real game-state agreement |\n| Dev/authoring | Live slider changes take effect and reset correctly; modified results labeled; illegal session cheats blocked; fault injection/recovery; valid editor export/import/playtest and invalid-content diagnostics |\n| Packaging and records | Root-deliverable HTML loads without the development tree on file URLs; loopback HTTP and embedded Hub play; single script ordering; no accidental development imports or audio files; settings/progress recovery; required report schema, feedback order, catalogue values, and credits consistency |\n\nThe official Hub harness uses a simulated network case with `players=4`, `latency=150`, `jitter=50`, and `loss=5`; use those documented values along with host-leave and server scenarios. Read the current harness instructions when assembling fixtures. Harness success establishes protocol compliance; your playthrough and state comparisons establish that cooperation actually works.\n\nUse a deterministic playthrough driver that chooses targets and issues ordinary actions through the same command/input path as a player. Keep any omniscient solver strictly in test/dev support; its purpose is repeatability, not automatic production gameplay. At least one complete acceptance run must navigate the actual player-facing controls, infer codes from revealed evidence, and choose replacements from available diagnostic information. Use fresh state for acceptance rather than relying on a developer-unlocked save.\n\nUse screenshots for visual questions scripts cannot settle: title appeal, active-board hierarchy, legibility at 10 feet, component distinction, cursor visibility, shader/lighting composition, large captions, fire-state readability, touch layout, and the success/failure payoff. Review representative frames from all three cases. A state assertion alone cannot establish that tools or clues are visible; a screenshot alone cannot establish that a disposal works. If a WebGPU screenshot is blank, use supported engine capture paths and state evidence, and report the rendering limitation accurately.\n\nKeep test-discovered issues, unique defect IDs, severity, fixes, and the check that found each one in `docs/testing.md`. This makes the counts and most/least helpful test answers evidence-based. Record browser/runtime versions actually used and distinguish browser emulation from unavailable hardware or listening checks. Never invent testing results to fill a report.\n\n13. Keep execution local, owned, and reproducible.\n\nHonor the shared workstation constraints. Make every development file, cache, dependency checkout, fixture, screenshot, and log inside your own assigned workspace/development folder. Read only relevant files; use `gh`/`git` for the referenced repositories and avoid neighboring agents' folders. Use installed tools before adding anything. Keep large build material off the shared RAM-backed temporary filesystem.\n\nPrefer file URLs and engine headless tools. For the Hub harness or another necessary HTTP check, start a loopback-only server on an OS-selected port, record its PID, and confirm the page's game identity before trusting results. A test fixture can serve the final bundle under its required filename without copying a second uncontrolled artifact. Stop only processes you started, by recorded PID. Keep one or two browser processes open at once and close them when finished; four harness frames can share a browser. Use installed Playwright 1.63.0 and its downloaded browsers rather than reinstalling them. Node tooling should stay project-local; follow the workstation's package-script approval behavior if a dependency requires it.\n\nTreat visual detail, lights, effects, and shadows as artistically unconstrained as the brief requests. Add what the scene needs. FPS targets and optimization work are outside this plan. Functional loading, correct state, browser compatibility, bounded process ownership, and cleanup still need verification.\n\n14. Finish the exact deliverable and its honest reports.\n\nDevelop modularly, then bundle the engine, game code, content, styles, and required original assets into the **single HTML artifact named and located by your own brief**. Inline the Hub client byte-for-byte as its mandated first script, outside minification or string rewriting. Test that the extracted inline client matches the vendored bytes or hash. Preserve dependency licenses. Escape script-closing sequences safely in subsequent bundled content. Avoid local module fetches or sibling assets that make the artifact depend on the development tree. Permitted CDN dependencies must have pinned versions and intentional loading/failure behavior; embedding the engine and game content makes ordinary solo sharing more reliable.\n\nSmoke-test the actual exported artifact on file URLs, via loopback HTTP, and inside the Hub harness after bundling. Verify its title, all three cases, loading, audio activation, input, credits, and exact session metadata. Inspect the final bundle for unintended debug auto-start, development URLs, audio files, and dependencies on private paths. Keep deliberate pause-accessible developer mode available.\n\nGenerate About/Credits, the required machine-readable builder exit report, and the final chat feedback from one truthful data source. Use the exact report filename, object shape, question order, catalogue fields, and allowed values in the builder's own brief. Fill all Source Information fields, including the actual supplied plan basename, source, genre, perspective, stack and versions, art, animation, procedural audio, online features, allowed testing, research, environment, and model. Copy run-specific metadata from the builder's brief. Usage is exactly `added on import`; unavailable facts are `unknown`.\n\nInclude all four required groups in **both** the in-game About/Credits screen and final chat reply: Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback. Keep the in-game presentation paginated, readable, and controller navigable. Answer each feedback question in its original order using the observed issue ledger and actual engine experience. Describe the implemented network features and the boundary of standalone connectivity testing. Report engine modifications explicitly, or `none` when none were made. Parse the final report as JSON and validate its schema and catalogue values against the brief.\n\nFinish when a fresh player can learn, repair, infer, cooperate, recover, win, lose, replay, and inspect the complete demo; the next case can be authored through the delivered data/tools; all required checks pass on the exported HTML; and every required deliverable and feedback surface is complete. Resolve all placeholders, missing menu destinations, inactive controls, and stubbed features before reporting completion.\nSOURCE: Bomb Squad - 1982\n\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\n\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\n\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\n\nResearch: Research the source before you start: its mechanics, levels, enemies, look and sound.\n\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-045026].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\n\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff. You may change anything in it, the engine included, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\n\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\n\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\n\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\n\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\n\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-045026] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-045026].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\n\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\n\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check.\n\nEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\n\n## This computer\nChecked 2026-11-05 19:08 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\n\n- **Computer:** IVY-DESK-1747k: Intel(R) Core(TM) i7-14700F, 20 cores, 16 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.2-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\n- **Space:** 757 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 7.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 7.7 GB free when checked.\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (no WebGL), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\n  Python's Playwright is in its own environment, not the system Python (python3 can't import it): run scripts that drive a browser with `/home/micha/.local/share/pipx/venvs/playwright/bin/python script.py`, or `uv run --with playwright==1.63.0 python script.py`.\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\n  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\n- **Tools:** node 24.21.0, npm 12.1.0, python3 3.14.4, pip3 25.1.1, git 2.53.0, gh 2.101.0, curl 8.18.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.46.1, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.98.1, cargo 1.98.1, go 1.27.1, java 21.0.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, sox 14.4.2, bun 1.3.14, deno 2.9.7, uv 0.12.18, tsc 5.9.3, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1, stable.\n- **Not installed:** lighthouse, shot-scraper, esbuild.\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\n\n### What this computer doesn't have\n\nNo script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\n\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\n\n### Sharing this computer\n\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\n- The system Python has no packages. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\n\n## Goal\n\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\n\nScope\n\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\n\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\n\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\n\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\n\nNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\n\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\n\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\n\nTouch-only devices: to keep the screen clear bare minimum or no UI (other than a menu button), invisible thumb sticks, multitouch, automate/assist with everything you need to accommodate the challenges of mobile/touch controls, use contact sensitive pop action and button on screen while possible\n\n## Done\nNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\n\n## Source Information\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n- Date: 2026-11-06 04:50:26\n- Source: the work it's based on\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\n- Art Direction: style used\n- Animation: system used\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\n- Online: network features used, or none\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\n- Research: research done, or none\n- Environment: Codex CLI (headless); Linux\n- Model: OpenAI, gpt-6.1-sol, reasoning effort ultra\n- Usage: write \"added on import\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\n\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\n{\n  \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\": \"\", \"source\": \"\", \"plan\": \"\", \"genre\": \"\", \"perspective\": \"\", \"tech\": \"\", \"artDirection\": \"\", \"animation\": \"\", \"audio\": \"\", \"online\": \"\", \"testing\": \"\", \"research\": \"\", \"environment\": \"\", \"model\": \"\", \"usage\": \"added on import\"},\n  \"testingFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"engineFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"promptFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"aiHub\": {\n    \"name\": \"the game's title, as on its title screen\",\n    \"tagline\": \"a few words to go under the title\",\n    \"description\": \"one or two sentences for the catalogue\",\n    \"type\": \"Game, Tech demo or Tool\",\n    \"genre\": \"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\",\n    \"source\": \"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\",\n    \"series\": \"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\",\n    \"players\": \"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\",\n    \"controls\": \"the controls, in one line\",\n    \"tags\": [\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\"]\n  }\n}\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\n\nTesting Feedback\n\n- How many issues did testing catch?\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\n\nEngine Feedback\n\n- What did you like most about the engine?\n- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n\nPrompt Feedback\n\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\n- What was confusing?\n- What was the hardest thing to do?\n- What would you change in this prompt?\n---\nname: game-toolchain\ndescription: Blender/Godot/texture/audio/tilemap/font toolchain on this box and its headless gotchas — use for any 3D, 2D, sprite, audio or Godot asset work\n---\n\n# Game asset creation toolchain (installed 2026-09-16 — do NOT reinstall)\n\nAll userspace, all driveable headlessly by an agent. Check `command -v` first.\n\n- **3D / Blender:** `blender` 5.2.2 LTS (`~/.local/opt/blender`, symlinked). Headless:\n  `blender --background --factory-startup --python script.py`. Cycles **GPU works via CUDA**\n  (RTX 4070 SUPER); OptiX is unavailable under WSL — set `compute_device_type='CUDA'`.\n  Blender as a *library* instead: `bpy-python -c \"import bpy; ...\"` (venv `~/.venvs/bpy`, cp313 wheel).\n- **3D mesh/CLI:** `assimp` (40+ format convert), `draco_encoder`, `gltfpack`, `gltf-transform`,\n  `gltf-pipeline`, `obj2gltf`, `gltf_validator` (Khronos; `-o` = JSON to stdout — don't merge stderr).\n- **Textures:** `magick` (IM7), `oiiotool` (OpenImageIO: EXR/mips), `toktx` + `ktx` (KTX2/Basis —\n  **`ktx create` takes INPUT then OUTPUT**), `basisu`, `cwebp`, `avifenc`, `heif-enc`,\n  optimizers `oxipng` `pngquant` `jpegoptim` `gifsicle` `zopflipng`.\n- **Vector:** `resvg`, `rsvg-convert` (SVG→raster), `potrace` (raster→SVG), `svgo` (npm).\n- **2D / sprites:** `pack-atlas` (helper — wraps free-tex-packer-cli; exporters incl. Godot, Phaser 3,\n  Unity3D, Spine: `pack-atlas sprites/*.png -o out -e \"Godot (atlas)\"`), `libresprite` (pixel art;\n  batch `libresprite -b in.png --sheet out.png --data out.json`), `rembg i in.png out.png` (cutouts).\n- **Tilemaps:** `tiled`, `tmxrasterizer` (map→PNG), `tmxviewer`, `terraingenerator`.\n- **Fonts:** `msdf-atlas-gen` (SDF/MSDF atlases for GPU text), `fonttools` / `ttx` / `pyftsubset`.\n- **Audio:** `sox` (procedural SFX: `sox -n hit.wav synth 0.2 sine 300-900 fade t 0.01 0.2 0.15`),\n  `ffmpeg`, `fluidsynth` + GM soundfont at `~/.local/share/soundfonts/MuseScore_General.sf3`,\n  `lame` `flac` `opusenc` `oggenc` `wavpack`.\n  ⚠ this `ffmpeg` has **no libvorbis** — for Ogg Vorbis use `sox in.wav out.ogg` or `oggenc` (16-bit input).\n- **Engine:** `godot` 4.7.2 + **export templates installed**. Headless:\n  `godot --headless --import`, `godot --headless --export-release \"Linux\" build/game.x86_64`.\n- **Python stack:** `gamedev-python` → venv `~/.venvs/gamedev` (trimesh, pymeshlab, open3d, xatlas,\n  pygltflib, meshio, usd-core/`pxr`, MaterialX, Pillow, OpenCV, scikit-image, shapely, rectpack,\n  librosa, soundfile, fontTools, numpy/scipy). Shims on PATH: `trimesh` `meshio` `open3d` `ttx` …\n\n**Headless gotchas already handled by the wrappers in `~/.local/bin` — don't \"fix\" them:**\n- Tiled bundles only Qt's `xcb` plugin → wrappers auto-run under `xvfb-run` when `$DISPLAY` is unset.\n- LibreSprite needs an SDL target even for `-b` → wrapper sets `SDL_VIDEODRIVER=dummy` when headless.\n- pymeshlab/open3d need `libOpenGL.so.0` + `libusb` → `~/.local/lib` (libglvnd extracted from the\n  Ubuntu .deb, no sudo) + brew lib are put on `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` by the venv wrappers.\n- Never symlink a venv `python` into `~/.local/bin` — it breaks `sys.prefix`. Use an `exec` wrapper.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
