{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat TASK.md'","aggregated_output":"SOURCE: Bomb Squad - 1982\n\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\n\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\n\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\n\nResearch: Research the source before you start: its mechanics, levels, enemies, look and sound.\n\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-045026].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\n\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff. You may change anything in it, the engine included, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\n\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\n\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\n\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\n\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\n\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-045026] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-045026].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\n\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\n\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check.\n\nEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\n\n## This computer\nChecked 2026-11-05 19:08 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\n\n- **Computer:** IVY-DESK-1747k: Intel(R) Core(TM) i7-14700F, 20 cores, 16 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.2-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\n- **Space:** 757 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 7.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 7.7 GB free when checked.\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (no WebGL), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\n  Python's Playwright is in its own environment, not the system Python (python3 can't import it): run scripts that drive a browser with `/home/micha/.local/share/pipx/venvs/playwright/bin/python script.py`, or `uv run --with playwright==1.63.0 python script.py`.\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\n  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\n- **Tools:** node 24.21.0, npm 12.1.0, python3 3.14.4, pip3 25.1.1, git 2.53.0, gh 2.101.0, curl 8.18.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.46.1, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.98.1, cargo 1.98.1, go 1.27.1, java 21.0.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, sox 14.4.2, bun 1.3.14, deno 2.9.7, uv 0.12.18, tsc 5.9.3, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1, stable.\n- **Not installed:** lighthouse, shot-scraper, esbuild.\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\n\n### What this computer doesn't have\n\nNo script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\n\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\n\n### Sharing this computer\n\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\n- The system Python has no packages. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\n\n## Goal\n\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\n\nScope\n\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\n\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\n\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\n\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\n\nNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\n\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\n\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\n\nTouch-only devices: to keep the screen clear bare minimum or no UI (other than a menu button), invisible thumb sticks, multitouch, automate/assist with everything you need to accommodate the challenges of mobile/touch controls, use contact sensitive pop action and button on screen while possible\n\n## Done\nNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\n\n## Source Information\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n- Date: 2026-11-06 04:50:26\n- Source: the work it's based on\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\n- Art Direction: style used\n- Animation: system used\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\n- Online: network features used, or none\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\n- Research: research done, or none\n- Environment: Codex CLI (headless); Linux\n- Model: OpenAI, gpt-6.1-sol, reasoning effort ultra\n- Usage: write \"added on import\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\n\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\n{\n  \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\": \"\", \"source\": \"\", \"plan\": \"\", \"genre\": \"\", \"perspective\": \"\", \"tech\": \"\", \"artDirection\": \"\", \"animation\": \"\", \"audio\": \"\", \"online\": \"\", \"testing\": \"\", \"research\": \"\", \"environment\": \"\", \"model\": \"\", \"usage\": \"added on import\"},\n  \"testingFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"engineFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"promptFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"aiHub\": {\n    \"name\": \"the game's title, as on its title screen\",\n    \"tagline\": \"a few words to go under the title\",\n    \"description\": \"one or two sentences for the catalogue\",\n    \"type\": \"Game, Tech demo or Tool\",\n    \"genre\": \"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\",\n    \"source\": \"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\",\n    \"series\": \"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\",\n    \"players\": \"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\",\n    \"controls\": \"the controls, in one line\",\n    \"tags\": [\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\"]\n  }\n}\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\n\nTesting Feedback\n\n- How many issues did testing catch?\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\n\nEngine Feedback\n\n- What did you like most about the engine?\n- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n\nPrompt Feedback\n\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\n- What was confusing?\n- What was the hardest thing to do?\n- What would you change in this prompt?\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '155,350p' PLAN.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '155,350p' PLAN.md\"","aggregated_output":"| `src/net/` | Session abstraction, Hub adapter, standalone peer adapter, state codec |\n| `src/dev/` | Live tuning, board/case editor, validation, inspection, test controls |\n| `tests/`, `tools/` | Simulation tests, real-input playthrough driver, audio checks, Hub fixtures, bundling and verification |\n| `docs/`, `evidence/` | Research, controls, authoring notes, requirement coverage, observed issues and test evidence |\n\nKeep the simulation independent of the renderer, browser input, audio, and transport. Presentation consumes state and events; it never mutates the puzzle during drawing. Use the engine's fixed-step facilities and a clearly defined clock policy rather than creating competing update loops. Verify disposal and scene-transition behavior, including the engine's timer-clearing rules.\n\nA case definition should contain its ID, brief, theme, digit count, glyph/code source, evidence sectors, repair-board references, time/workload per party size, dialogue cues, introduced mechanics, and ending beats. A board template should contain component positions and terminal anchors, required job order, replacement rule, spare tray, hazard settings, and evidence reward. IDs remain stable across rendering, saves, networking, tests, and editor exports. Content variation is seeded and versioned.\n\nExpose developer mode through pause. Provide case/seed selection, restart/checkpoint, single-step and time scale for solo testing, fuse adjustment, component/state inspection, fault/fire injection, reveal/solve actions, input display, and network status. Clearly mark modified runs and isolate them from ordinary records. Hub sessions retain local inspection and presentation settings; gameplay-changing cheats, tuning, and editor controls are disabled in live session play. Standalone cooperative test tuning requires host authority and broadcasts its revision to all peers.\n\nUse native settings sliders with live application and reset-to-defaults. Expose fuse duration/time scale, tool travel/precision assistance, solder safe-band/heat gain/cooling, recovery-window length, penalty size, fire intensity/extinguish rate, and hint delay. Define what happens to an in-progress action when its setting changes. Keep ranges finite and safe; resetting restores authored defaults coherently. Relevant native APIs include `settings.define` with slider type and an `apply` function, and `settings.reset(group)`.\n\nProvide a small practical board/case authoring panel in dev mode: load a template, place/select components and terminal anchors, order required repairs, choose rule and spares, paint evidence masks on the digit stencil, set case pacing, validate, and playtest a scratch copy. Support versioned JSON import/export with explicit save feedback. Keep game records and original templates safe from scratch editing. Include a short authoring guide and demonstrate adding a small valid board through this workflow. This panel serves the narrow content model; it does not need to become a general-purpose level editor.\n\nVersion local settings and progress saves. Save case unlocks, best results, assistance state, and replay seeds at safe boundaries. Treat unavailable or corrupted storage as recoverable and continue play with defaults. Hub's isolated storage must not contaminate ordinary progress. Keep an issue ledger as testing proceeds so final feedback can cite actual discovered defects and engine changes.\n\n10. Make attract mode a convincing, isolated demonstration.\n\nAfter **10 seconds without input on the standalone title/menu**, start a looping AI-driven sequence that uses the actual repair simulation and presentation. Use the native `E.Arcade` idle/attract support where it fits; verify its exact lifecycle. Show a complete cut/grip/place/solder repair, a shape/color test, a recoverable fire, evidence discovery, a code submission, and the city payoff across a short rotating playlist. The attract actor may cheat as permitted, but its actions must visibly demonstrate the mechanics.\n\nAttract mode owns a separate local state, seed, records context, and audio lifecycle. It never joins a room, calls session readiness, writes progress, or changes settings. Display a large invitation and a clear demo indicator. Any controller, keyboard, mouse, or touch input exits immediately to a stable menu; consume that wake input so it does not also submit a choice or tool action. Reset idle tracking on genuine input, not noisy gamepad axes. Do not interrupt text entry, active online lobbies, developer editing, or active Hub cards with attract play.\n\n11. Implement through integrated completion gates.\n\nUse these gates in order, with presentation, controls, and testing accompanying the relevant system. Each gate produces working game behavior; required features remain part of the final delivery.\n\n| Gate | Work to complete | Evidence needed to proceed |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Source and contracts | Research ledger, title procedure, engine/Hub versions, chosen projection, input actions, state/command/content schemas, miniature engine and transport proofs | Verified primary references; authentic native rendering/animation; a real standalone peer link; a Hub authority without a local player |\n| Complete repair loop | One attractive board, all physical tools, correct order, full repair state machine, diagnosis, recovery, heat/fire, initial evidence and code submission | Real controller and mouse paths can complete and fail the board; state tests cover legal and illegal transitions |\n| First finished case | Briefing, readable HUD, original scene/assets/animations, procedural cues/voices, pause/settings, win/loss/retry/results | A complete normal-input case, including deliberate recoverable error and early deduction; 10-foot visual review |\n| Shared play | Same simulation through Hub and standalone adapters, ownership, sync, confirmation, disconnect lifecycle, party scaling | Two real peers dispose a device; Hub harness scenarios pass; authority-only server works; no leaked non-Hub networking |\n| Full authored slice | Training, all three cases, campaign ending, case select/replay, every mechanic's taught and combined use, saves | Whole-campaign scripted playthrough using ordinary commands; all content validates; no missing progression or scene |\n| Production tools and presentation | Dev tools/sliders/reset, editor/import/export, attract playlist, touch adaptation, complete original audiovisual feedback, About/Credits | Editor-authored content round-trips; attract wakes cleanly; every input reaches every required screen |\n| Delivery | Single-HTML bundle, standalone and Hub smoke checks against that artifact, final feedback/report data, credits and licenses | Final acceptance matrix passes; report parses and matches actual evidence; output names and locations match the builder's own brief |\n\nIf an engine abstraction gets in the way, modify it with a recorded reason and a focused regression check. Keep the changes small enough to understand in the context of the whole game. Use the large context to audit cross-system assumptions before they become late integration failures: pause versus host time, animations versus authoritative repairs, masks versus glyphs, tools versus touch, packaging versus client injection, and feedback data versus actual test records.\n\n12. Verify behavior, then verify the final artifact.\n\nPut all automated checks behind **`node tools/verify.mjs`**. It should print only failures and one final count line, exit nonzero on failure, and support focused groups for changed areas. Record results in machine-readable evidence files while keeping console output quiet. Rerun a group when its covered code or content changes. Run the aggregate after the final relevant changes. Wrap any noisy native tools rather than losing their error information.\n\nUse the engine's documented headless tools, including its headless game mode, deterministic advance, input feeding, snapshots, error collection, and disposal. Its handoff also supplies checking, replay, filmstrip, and asset-sheet tools. Confirm signatures from the version used. Use real browser tests for input, shell, rendering lifecycle, transport, audio activation, and bundling. Tests should establish behavior and invariants rather than duplicate implementation logic.\n\n| Verification area | Required checks |\n| --- | --- |\n| Repair model | Every valid state transition; both terminals required; wrong order/cut/part recovery; dropped parts; safe solder band; overheating/fire/extinguishing; leaving and revisiting a partial board; duplicate commands; no stranded stock or stuck holds |\n| Code and content | Distinct actual glyphs; lit/dark/unknown evidence; consistent reveal packs; proof of unique guaranteed solution for authored cases and generated variants; useful early inference; deliberate wrong submission; all IDs and orders valid |\n| Time and outcomes | Single terminal outcome at deadline boundaries; solo pause/resume; online menus; time scale/reset; background/resume; fire and heat while away from a board; retry seed fidelity |\n| Full playthrough | Ordinary controls from fresh start through training, all cases, ending, results, case replay, and credits; exercise every mechanic; include recovery and separate time-expiry/wrong-code failure routes; no direct state writes or invulnerability in the acceptance run |\n| Controller | Standard-mapped gamepad stub via browser initialization; focus, repeat, tool changes, precision, submission, pause, dev sliders, glyph switching, disconnect/reconnect, and no accidental wake action |\n| Keyboard/mouse | Hover moves focus; click acts on hovered target; keyboard-only menu reachability; expected keys; wheel/tools; rebinding labels; overlays restore focus; input-device changes update prompts |\n| Touch | Phone/tablet profiles, taps, drags, multitouch, contextual actions, assisted solder, orientation/reflow, and reachable menu/settings; clearly record these as emulated checks |\n| Audio | User activation, correct default music gain of 0.1, mute/bus controls, finite nonzero synthesis, voice priority, caption completeness, no repeated snapshot barks, no continuing sound after Hub end |\n| Attract | Starts after ten idle seconds; loops the full playlist; any input exits; wake event consumed; no save/score/settings changes; no network side effects; no active-play interruption |\n| Standalone network | Actual shipped host/join path in two pages; version mismatch/full/unavailable room; several repairs by different seats; canonical-state agreement; shared code result; disconnect and snapshot rejoin; owned-peer cleanup; failed signaling/NAT state is actionable |\n| Hub network | Official automatic checks, normal two-player round, documented four-player latency/jitter/loss case, host-leave, time-up/end, and `scenario=server`; assert `window.__hubCheck.pass === true` and the harness completion marker, then also assert real game-state agreement |\n| Dev/authoring | Live slider changes take effect and reset correctly; modified results labeled; illegal session cheats blocked; fault injection/recovery; valid editor export/import/playtest and invalid-content diagnostics |\n| Packaging and records | Root-deliverable HTML loads without the development tree on file URLs; loopback HTTP and embedded Hub play; single script ordering; no accidental development imports or audio files; settings/progress recovery; required report schema, feedback order, catalogue values, and credits consistency |\n\nThe official Hub harness uses a simulated network case with `players=4`, `latency=150`, `jitter=50`, and `loss=5`; use those documented values along with host-leave and server scenarios. Read the current harness instructions when assembling fixtures. Harness success establishes protocol compliance; your playthrough and state comparisons establish that cooperation actually works.\n\nUse a deterministic playthrough driver that chooses targets and issues ordinary actions through the same command/input path as a player. Keep any omniscient solver strictly in test/dev support; its purpose is repeatability, not automatic production gameplay. At least one complete acceptance run must navigate the actual player-facing controls, infer codes from revealed evidence, and choose replacements from available diagnostic information. Use fresh state for acceptance rather than relying on a developer-unlocked save.\n\nUse screenshots for visual questions scripts cannot settle: title appeal, active-board hierarchy, legibility at 10 feet, component distinction, cursor visibility, shader/lighting composition, large captions, fire-state readability, touch layout, and the success/failure payoff. Review representative frames from all three cases. A state assertion alone cannot establish that tools or clues are visible; a screenshot alone cannot establish that a disposal works. If a WebGPU screenshot is blank, use supported engine capture paths and state evidence, and report the rendering limitation accurately.\n\nKeep test-discovered issues, unique defect IDs, severity, fixes, and the check that found each one in `docs/testing.md`. This makes the counts and most/least helpful test answers evidence-based. Record browser/runtime versions actually used and distinguish browser emulation from unavailable hardware or listening checks. Never invent testing results to fill a report.\n\n13. Keep execution local, owned, and reproducible.\n\nHonor the shared workstation constraints. Make every development file, cache, dependency checkout, fixture, screenshot, and log inside your own assigned workspace/development folder. Read only relevant files; use `gh`/`git` for the referenced repositories and avoid neighboring agents' folders. Use installed tools before adding anything. Keep large build material off the shared RAM-backed temporary filesystem.\n\nPrefer file URLs and engine headless tools. For the Hub harness or another necessary HTTP check, start a loopback-only server on an OS-selected port, record its PID, and confirm the page's game identity before trusting results. A test fixture can serve the final bundle under its required filename without copying a second uncontrolled artifact. Stop only processes you started, by recorded PID. Keep one or two browser processes open at once and close them when finished; four harness frames can share a browser. Use installed Playwright 1.63.0 and its downloaded browsers rather than reinstalling them. Node tooling should stay project-local; follow the workstation's package-script approval behavior if a dependency requires it.\n\nTreat visual detail, lights, effects, and shadows as artistically unconstrained as the brief requests. Add what the scene needs. FPS targets and optimization work are outside this plan. Functional loading, correct state, browser compatibility, bounded process ownership, and cleanup still need verification.\n\n14. Finish the exact deliverable and its honest reports.\n\nDevelop modularly, then bundle the engine, game code, content, styles, and required original assets into the **single HTML artifact named and located by your own brief**. Inline the Hub client byte-for-byte as its mandated first script, outside minification or string rewriting. Test that the extracted inline client matches the vendored bytes or hash. Preserve dependency licenses. Escape script-closing sequences safely in subsequent bundled content. Avoid local module fetches or sibling assets that make the artifact depend on the development tree. Permitted CDN dependencies must have pinned versions and intentional loading/failure behavior; embedding the engine and game content makes ordinary solo sharing more reliable.\n\nSmoke-test the actual exported artifact on file URLs, via loopback HTTP, and inside the Hub harness after bundling. Verify its title, all three cases, loading, audio activation, input, credits, and exact session metadata. Inspect the final bundle for unintended debug auto-start, development URLs, audio files, and dependencies on private paths. Keep deliberate pause-accessible developer mode available.\n\nGenerate About/Credits, the required machine-readable builder exit report, and the final chat feedback from one truthful data source. Use the exact report filename, object shape, question order, catalogue fields, and allowed values in the builder's own brief. Fill all Source Information fields, including the actual supplied plan basename, source, genre, perspective, stack and versions, art, animation, procedural audio, online features, allowed testing, research, environment, and model. Copy run-specific metadata from the builder's brief. Usage is exactly `added on import`; unavailable facts are `unknown`.\n\nInclude all four required groups in **both** the in-game About/Credits screen and final chat reply: Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback. Keep the in-game presentation paginated, readable, and controller navigable. Answer each feedback question in its original order using the observed issue ledger and actual engine experience. Describe the implemented network features and the boundary of standalone connectivity testing. Report engine modifications explicitly, or `none` when none were made. Parse the final report as JSON and validate its schema and catalogue values against the brief.\n\nFinish when a fresh player can learn, repair, infer, cooperate, recover, win, lose, replay, and inspect the complete demo; the next case can be authored through the delivered data/tools; all required checks pass on the exported HTML; and every required deliverable and feedback surface is complete. Resolve all placeholders, missing menu destinations, inactive controls, and stubbed features before reporting completion.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'ls -la; for file in /home/AGENTS.md /home/micha/AGENTS.md /home/micha/dev/AGENTS.md /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/AGENTS.md; do if [ -f \"$file\" ]; then printf '\"'%s\\\\n' \\\"\"'$file\"; cat \"$file\"; fi; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'ls -la; for file in /home/AGENTS.md /home/micha/AGENTS.md /home/micha/dev/AGENTS.md /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/AGENTS.md; do if [ -f \"$file\" ]; then printf '\"'%s\\\\n' \\\"\"'$file\"; cat \"$file\"; fi; done'","aggregated_output":"total 72\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 micha micha  4096 Nov  6 04:50 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 4 micha micha  4096 Nov  6 04:50 ..\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha 49007 Nov  6 04:50 PLAN.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha 12500 Nov  6 04:50 TASK.md\n/home/micha/AGENTS.md\n# AGENTS.md — Workstation environment for AI coding agents\n\n> Home-level instructions for any coding agent (Claude Code, Codex, Gemini, opencode, grok, agy, aider)\n> on this box. Linked as every agent's *global* rules file, so it applies inside any repo; repo-level\n> `AGENTS.md` files override it. Only things you **cannot infer**. Keep < 10 000 chars (grok truncates).\n> Last optimized: 2026-09-23. Full history/rationale: `~/AI-DEV-SETUP.md`.\n\n## Environment (the non-obvious bits)\n\n- **Ubuntu 26.04 on WSL2**, 20 cores, **16 GB RAM cap** (`.wslconfig`). **`/tmp` is a 7.9 GB RAM tmpfs** —\n  put big builds/caches under the project or `~/.cache`, never `/tmp`.\n- **No Linux GUI** (WSLg disabled on purpose). GUI checks: `xvfb-run -a`; Android: headless emulator +\n  screenshots. **Don't launch GUI `.exe`s** via interop — they pop up on the user's desktop.\n- **sudo requires a password** (ask Michael — never guess/hardcode). Default to userspace installs:\n  `mise use -g <tool>@latest` · `uv tool install <pkg>` · `npm i -g <pkg>` · `cargo binstall <crate>`.\n\n## PATH — one file, every shell\n\n- **`~/.config/ai-dev/env.sh`** is the single source of PATH/toolchain env, sourced last by `~/.profile`\n  (so `bash -lc` agents get it) and `~/.bashrc`. Add PATH entries **there**, not in rc files.\n- Precedence: `~/.local/bin` > mise shims > JDK 21 > nvm node > cargo/bun/dotnet/flutter/android/go\n  > system > **Homebrew last** (brew duplicates ~200 system bins — python3, git, glib tools — and once\n  hijacked `node`; never `brew install node`, never prepend brew).\n- **Windows PATH is filtered to an allowlist** (system32, PowerShell, WindowsApps, `code`, `cursor`,\n  `gsudo`); Windows dev-tool shims otherwise shadow missing Linux tools. Windows toolchains:\n  `cmd.exe /c \"<cmd>\"` or `powershell.exe -NoProfile -Command \"<cmd>\"`. Full PATH: `AI_DEV_KEEP_WINPATH=1`.\n- Pagers are neutralized for agents (`PAGER=cat` etc.). `rg` is pre-configured (`~/.ripgreprc`).\n\n## Installed toolchain — do NOT reinstall (check `command -v` first)\n\n- **Runtimes:** node **24 LTS** (nvm default; 22 kept), bun, deno, python 3.14, go 1.27, rust 1.98,\n  **JDK 21** (`JAVA_HOME`=brew `openjdk@21`; brew's `java` 27 is shadowed on purpose), kotlin, gradle,\n  maven, **.NET 10** (`~/.dotnet`), **Flutter 3.47/Dart 3.13** (`~/.local/opt/flutter`), zig, gcc 15,\n  clang 21 (system; brew LLVM 23 only for `clangd`/`clang-tidy`), cmake, ninja, meson.\n- **Mobile/desktop:** Android SDK `~/Android/Sdk` (API 36+37, build-tools 37, NDK 30, emulator, AVD\n  `pixel8_api37`, **`android` CLI**), Chrome, `eas`, `tauri`, `maestro`, `cargo-xwin`, mingw-w64, nsis,\n  GTK/WebKitGTK dev libs, rust targets (android, windows-gnu/msvc, wasm32).\n  → **Read `~/.claude/skills/app-platforms/SKILL.md` before any mobile or desktop app work.**\n- **Pkg/env mgrs:** `uv` (prefer over pip), `pipx`, pnpm/yarn (corepack), `mise`, `cargo binstall`.\n- **Search/nav:** `rg`, `fd`, `ast-grep` (alias **`sg` shadows the system `sg`** — use `/usr/bin/sg`),\n  `fzf`, `zoxide`, `ctags`.\n- **Data/HTTP:** `jq`, `gron`, `xh`, `curl`, `7zz`, `parallel`. ⚠ **two `yq`s:** `yq` = python/kislyuk\n  4.x (jq syntax, `-y` for YAML out); the Go/mikefarah one is **`yq-go`** (`yq-go -o=json '.a'`).\n- **QA:** `ruff`, `ty`, `biome`, `eslint`, `oxlint`, `prettier`, `shellcheck`, `shfmt`, `pre-commit`,\n  `semgrep`, `tokei`, `hyperfine`, `knip`, `cspell`.\n- **Browser tests:** Playwright for **Python** (1.63) is a pipx venv: run scripts that `import playwright`\n  with `~/.local/share/pipx/venvs/playwright/bin/python` (system `python3` can't import it). Browsers are\n  in `~/.cache/ms-playwright`; if WebKit reports missing host libraries: `sudo ~/.local/bin/playwright install-deps webkit`.\n- **Other:** `repomix`, `files-to-prompt`, `llm`, `just`, `watchexec`, `sqlite3`, `psql`, `redis-cli`,\n  `pandoc`, `dot`, `direnv`, `git` 2.53 + `git-lfs`, `gh` (authenticated), `docker` (native engine,\n  user in group), `kubectl`, `helm`, `terraform`, `vercel`, `ollama` (GPU, qwen2.5-coder).\n- **Agents on PATH:** `claude`, `codex`, `gemini`, `opencode`, `grok`, `agy`, `aider`, `jules`.\n\n## Node / npm gotchas\n\n- **npm 12 blocks two things by default** (the install still \"succeeds\"):\n  (a) dependency **install scripts** — native/binary-downloading deps end up broken. Fix per project:\n  `npm approve-scripts <pkg>` (writes `allowScripts` in package.json) then `npm rebuild <pkg>`;\n  globals: `--allow-scripts=<pkg>` (allow-listed ones: `npm config get allow-scripts`).\n  (b) **git-URL dependencies** (`EALLOWGIT`) — e.g. Electron Forge. Fix: `allow-git=all` in the\n  project `.npmrc`; scaffolders: `npm_config_allow_git=all npx create-electron-app …`.\n- Keep `nvm alias default` an **exact** version (env.sh reads it without loading nvm). New node via\n  nvm starts with no globals: `nvm reinstall-packages <old>`. Don't `npm config set prefix`.\n- Global `tsc` is pinned to **5.9.3** (TS 7 ships no `tsserver`; `typescript-language-server` needs it).\n  The native 7.x compiler is `tsgo`. Repos pinning their own `typescript` are unaffected.\n\n## Working conventions\n\n- **Search:** lexical `rg` → structural `ast-grep --lang <l> -p '<pattern>'` → language LSP.\n- **GitHub/git: use `gh`/`git` CLIs, not MCP** (far fewer tokens, already authenticated).\n- **Python:** `uv run`, `uv add`, `ruff check --fix && ruff format`, `ty check`. No bare `pip`.\n- **JS/TS:** `biome check --write` when the repo has no formatter configured.\n- **Shell scripts you write:** `shellcheck` + `shfmt -w`. Never `pkill -f <pat>` when `<pat>` is in your\n  own command line — it kills your own shell.\n- **Verify before claiming done:** run the repo's own test/build commands (`justfile`, `package.json`\n  scripts, `Makefile`, `pyproject.toml`).\n- **Parallel work:** git worktrees (`gwt <branch>` in interactive shells, or `git worktree add`).\n\n## Language servers (all verified with a real LSP `initialize`, 2026-09-23)\n\n| Language | Server | Typecheck / compile |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| TS/JS | `typescript-language-server` | `tsc` 5.9.3, `tsgo`, `eslint`, `oxlint`, `biome` |\n| Python | `pyright-langserver` | `ty`, `mypy`, `pyright`; `ruff` lint/format |\n| Go · Rust · C/C++ | `gopls` · `rust-analyzer` · `clangd` | `go vet` · `cargo check` · gcc/clang, cmake |\n| Java · Kotlin | `jdtls` · `kotlin-lsp` | `javac`/gradle · `kotlinc`/gradle |\n| C# · Dart/Flutter | `csharp-ls` · `dart language-server` | `dotnet build` · `dart analyze`/`flutter analyze` |\n| Bash · YAML · Lua · Markdown | `bash-language-server` · `yaml-language-server` · `lua-language-server` · `marksman` | `shellcheck` · — · — · `markdownlint` |\n| JSON/CSS/HTML | `vscode-{json,css,html}-language-server` | `biome`, `stylelint` |\n\nIf `rust-analyzer` says *\"Unknown binary … in official toolchain\"*: `rustup component add rust-analyzer`.\n\n## Skills (shared across agents)\n\n- `~/.claude/skills/` is the source of truth (grok reads it directly); each skill is also linked into\n  `~/.codex/skills`, `~/.config/opencode/skills`, `~/.gemini/skills`, `~/.gemini/antigravity-cli/skills`.\n- **app-platforms** (mobile + desktop), **game-toolchain** (Blender/Godot/texture/audio — read before any\n  3D/2D/sprite/audio/Godot work), **android-cli** (official; refresh with `android init`).\n  Imagine art (sprites, animation, tiles, UI, GLB) is the bundled skill **game-assets**. Do not merge it with **game-toolchain**.\n\n## MCP servers — keep it lean\n\n- >10 MCP servers measurably degrades agents. Connected for Claude: **Context7** (live library docs —\n  use it instead of guessing current APIs) plus productivity integrations.\n- Add a coding MCP **only when a CLI can't do the job**, per project (e.g. browser automation:\n  `claude mcp add chrome-devtools -- npx -y chrome-devtools-mcp@latest`). Android UI inspection\n  needs no MCP: `android layout` / `android screen`.\n- MCP servers run as this user: pin versions, prefer vetted servers, keep a human in the loop for\n  writes/sends/deletes.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"I’m using the `game-toolchain` skill for the native game art workflow. I’ll establish the engine and network contracts first, then split the simulation, presentation, and cooperative-play work across agents.\n"}}
