{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"kill 1451477 1451487; rg --files vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits | head -30; rg -n 'class Shell|function Shell|class Input|class Settings' vendor/my-3d2dge.js; sed -n '45,180p' vendor/hub/README.md\"","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: line 1: kill: (1451487) - No such process\nvendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/rpg.html\nvendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/adventure.js\nvendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/rhythm.html\nvendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/README.md\nvendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/rts.js\nvendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/sports.html\nvendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/brawler.html\nvendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/road.html\nvendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/arcade.js\nvendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/test.mjs\nvendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/sim.html\nvendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/sports.js\nvendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/rhythm.js\nvendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/arcade.html\nvendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/tactics.html\nvendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/pinball.html\nvendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/roguelike.js\nvendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/animlab.html\nvendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/rts.html\nvendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/puzzle.html\nvendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/tactics.js\nvendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/platformer.html\nvendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/kart.js\nvendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/rpg.js\nvendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/metroidvania.html\nvendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/sim.js\nvendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/roguelike.html\nvendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/artillery.html\nvendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/platformer.js\nvendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/pinball.js\n754:class Input {\n3087:class Settings {\n9415:class Shell {\n`co-op` and `versus` as shared world.\n\n---\n\n## A round\n\n1. The hub loads the game on every player's device, and the players get 10\n   to 30 seconds with the game's card, longer if one asks for more time: what\n   the game is, how to win, the controls. The card is the hub's, from the\n   game's tags, or the game's own (*The card*).\n2. The game connects and calls `ready()`.\n3. The round starts on the hub's clock, on every device at once.\n4. The game reports how it goes.\n5. The round ends when every player has a result, when the host of a shared\n   world finishes it, or when the hub calls time. The hub shows the results\n   over the game, then removes it.\n\n### Tags\n\nIn the game's `<head>`:\n\n```html\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"MIN-MAX\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"MODE\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"GOAL\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"CONTROLS\">\n```\n\n- `hub:session-players`: the smallest and largest number of players the game\n  takes in a session. The hub deals the game only when the number of players\n  is in that range.\n- `hub:session-mode`: `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world`.\n- `hub:session-goal` and `hub:session-howto`: how to win and the game's\n  controls, shown on the hub's card.\n\nOptional:\n\n- `hub:session-minutes`: how many minutes a round of the game takes. The hub\n  shows it and uses it to decide when to call time.\n- `hub:webgpu` with `required`: the game cannot run without WebGPU. The hub\n  then deals it only when every player's browser has WebGPU.\n\n### The client\n\n[session.js](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js)\nis pasted into the game unmodified, as the first script in `<head>`, right\nafter `<meta charset>`. In a session it:\n\n- seeds `Math.random`, in parallel and ghost, before any of the game's own\n  scripts run, so it gives the same numbers on every copy;\n- gives the game an empty `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` of its own, so\n  nothing stored on the device carries into the round, and nothing the round\n  stores reaches the device's own storage;\n- hands `requestFullscreen()` and `exitFullscreen()` to the hub, which takes\n  its whole page fullscreen; `document.fullscreenElement` and\n  `fullscreenchange` follow the hub's page.\n\nThe hub replaces an unmodified copy of the client with each new version. An\nedited copy is not replaced.\n\n### Joining\n\n- `AIHub.active` is `true` from the first line when the hub opened the page.\n- In a session, the game opens straight into the round: no title screen,\n  menus or prompts before it, other than its own card (*The card*).\n- `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats: [min, max] })` returns a promise of the\n  round's `info`, or of `null` when there is no hub after all; with `null`\n  the game runs as it does on its own.\n- `AIHub.ready()` is called once, when the game has loaded and the round can\n  start, with no key press: the players answer the card.\n- The round starts when the card's countdown ends or every player pressed\n  Ready, and every game has called `ready()`. A game still loading then gets\n  20 more seconds; after that its player sits the round out.\n\n`info` (also on `AIHub` after `connect()`):\n\n| Field | |\n|---|---|\n| `me` | this copy's player id |\n| `players` | `[{ id, name, color }]`, ids `0..N-1` in seat order |\n| `host`, `isHost` | the id of the copy that runs a shared world, and whether it is this one |\n| `server` | `true` only on a copy that hosts a shared world with no player of its own |\n| `mode`, `round`, `limitMs` | the mode, the round number, and the round's hard limit after the start |\n| `seed` | a number, the same on every copy, behind `AIHub.rng(label)` |\n\nNames come from the hub and are never inserted into the page as HTML. Colours\nfollow the seat: `#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`.\n\n### The card\n\nBefore every round the players get 10 to 30 seconds to learn what comes\nnext and say when they are ready. A player can ask for more time, which adds\nten seconds; while more than half of them are asking, the countdown holds.\nBy default the hub shows its own card over the game. A game can make the\ncard its own, as part of the game:\n\n```js\nAIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card: true });\n```\n\nThe hub's card then steps aside when the game calls `ready()`, and the game's\nscreen is the card until `start`. How it looks and sounds is the game's: its\ntitle, its music, its world, the players lining up. Introduce the game the way\nit deserves.\n\nWhile it is on, the card shows:\n\n- the game's name;\n- the players, each in their colour, and which of them are ready;\n- how to win the round;\n- the controls this player will use;\n- the time left, from the `card` event.\n\nAnd it takes two answers, from the keyboard, a controller, or a tap or click\non the card:\n\n- **Ready**: Enter, Cross (button 0), or a tap on Ready:\n  `AIHub.answer('ready')`.\n- **More time**: Triangle (button 3), a key the card names, or a tap on More\n  time: `AIHub.answer('more')`. Answering `more` again takes it back.\n\nNothing done on the card changes the round. It has no menus, settings or\nchoices, and play begins only at `start`. In parallel and ghost it draws no\nnumbers from `Math.random`, which would change the round's start: use\n`AIHub.rng('card')`.\n\n`card` arrives whenever it changes, and every second while the countdown runs:\n\n| Field | |\n|---|---|\n| `left` | milliseconds until the round starts by itself; `null` while paused or starting |\n| `paused` | `true` while most players asked for more time |\n| `starting` | `true` once the countdown is over or everyone is ready: `start` follows when every game has loaded |\n| `ready` | the ids of the players who are ready |\n| `asking` | how many players asked for more time (never who) |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat docs/sim-contract.md docs/net-contract.md; sed -n '9415,9525p' vendor/my-3d2dge.js; rg -n 'standard\\\\(|_pause|screens.pause|\\\\.shell|_root|className|theme.scale' vendor/my-3d2dge.js | tail -35; sed -n '1,70p' vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/pointclick.js\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat docs/sim-contract.md docs/net-contract.md; sed -n '9415,9525p' vendor/my-3d2dge.js; rg -n 'standard\\\\(|_pause|screens.pause|\\\\.shell|_root|className|theme.scale' vendor/my-3d2dge.js | tail -35; sed -n '1,70p' vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/pointclick.js\"","aggregated_output":"# Bomb Union simulation contract v1\nScripts load `src/sim/content.js`, then `src/sim/model.js`. Both use `globalThis.BU` (no modules).\n\n`BU.CONTENT` is an object keyed by `training`, `waterfront`, `relay`, `central`. Each definition has id, title, subtitle, brief, theme, digits, boardCount, duration, intro, taunts, success, failure, mechanics. `BU.GLYPHS` is ten arrays of twenty 0/1 cells (4 columns x 5 rows). `BU.DEFAULTS` holds finite live tuning.\n\n`BU.Sim.create(caseId='waterfront', seed=1, party=1, tuning={})` returns a JSON-safe mutable authoritative state. Party can be count 1–4 or array of seat IDs. State has `caseId, seed, party, seats, duration, remaining, elapsed, revision, status` (`active`, `won`, `lost`), `cause, code` (array digits; dev/tests only), `evidence` (one array of 20 values -1 unknown, 0 dark, 1 lit per digit), `boards`, `events` (bounded newest accepted events with monotonic `id`, `type`, optional board/component and text), `metrics`, `proposal`, `tuning`, `modified`, `assisted`, and eventual `result`.\n\nEach board: `id` (B1...), `name`, `digit` (zero based), `mask` (reward cell indices), `rule` (`jumper`, `shape`, `color`), `ruleKnown` (boolean), `example` (source/replacement shape/color/pattern), `components`, `order` (required component IDs), `step`, `spares`, `held` (spare ID or null), `claim` (seat ID or null), `heat` (0–100+), `fire`, `recovery` ({component,terminal,remaining} or null), `hold` ({seat,tool,component,terminal,duration} or null), `complete`, `revision`. `components` have `id` C1..., `x,y` in world units around board origin, `shape` (`resistor`, `capacitor`, `chip`), `color` (`amber`, `coral`, `cyan`), `pattern`, `kind` (`jumper`, `replace`), `cuts` pair booleans, `removed`, `part` (spare ID or null), `joints` pair booleans, `done`. Terminal anchors are (x-18,y),(x+18,y). Native workspace may transform this origin freely. Spares: `id` S1..., shape, color, pattern, jumper boolean, available boolean. Each component has exactly one compatible spare; reusable after wrong installation/removal/drop. Stock never destroyed.\n\n`BU.Sim.command(state, seat, cmd)` mutates only authority, returns `{ok,reason,event}`. cmd `{type, board?, component?, terminal?, spare?, id?, caseId?, expectedRevision?}`. Optional `expectedRevision` checks board revision (or global for no board); an `id` deduplicates. Action types:\n- `claim`, `release`: board. Claim is atomic, one owned board per seat; claiming new board releases old claim and cancels hold without clearing damage/fire. `release` preserves all repair state.\n- `cut`: board, component, terminal 0/1. Both leads required. Off-order cut opens recovery; further cuts rejected while it is pending.\n- `grip`: board, component. Removes original after both cuts, or wrong replacement after its leads cut (wrong replacement initially unsoldered and immediately removable). Returns any installed spare to stock. Original is automatically salvaged, so no stranded junk-handling state.\n- `pick`: board, spare. Pliers pick available spare. Drop current held spare first.\n- `place`: board, component. Places held spare in empty current socket. Wrong part is installed, fails continuity, remains visibly removable. Rule compatibility checked without consuming stock.\n- `drop`: board. Returns held spare safely to stock.\n- `test`: board, component (defaults current), spare (defaults held). Costs default 2 fuse seconds and discovers fixed board rule; emits PASS/FAIL text, never consumes spare. Can test from tray or while held.\n- `begin`: board, tool=`solder` or `extinguish`, component, terminal. Solder on a cut wrong joint repairs recovery; otherwise on installed correct current socket. `end`: board. Solder release succeeds only within safe band [tuning.solderMin, tuning.solderMax]. Excess hold heats/fire. Extinguish release succeeds after tuning.extinguishTime. `cancel`: board clears active hold, no free repair. Holds auto-expire by tuning.maxHold; client loss calls cancel/release.\n- `propose`: code array. Requires two completed boards (one training); starts cooperative five-second vote; proposing counts yes. Solo immediately resolves (the UI must deliberately confirm before sending this). `vote`: accept boolean. Any veto cancels; unanimous immediate resolution; strict majority/no veto after deadline resolves; otherwise cancels.\n- `leave`: removes seat, releases claims/holds; cancels proposer departure; adjusts eligible vote seats. Party workload/fuse remains locked.\n- `tune`: values object. Clamps known tuning keys, cancels holds coherently, marks modified. Transport must prohibit in live Hub. `dev`: operation (`fire`,`fault`,`reveal`,`solve`,`fuse`) and board/value. Marks modified.\n\n`BU.Sim.advance(state, dt)` advances authoritative simulation time; caller implements solo pause and online wall-clock policy. Cools offscreen boards, maintains established fire, evaluates recovery penalties, expires bounded holds/proposals, and resolves fuse exactly once. `BU.Sim.instruction(state,boardId)` returns persistent text describing the next legal repair step. `BU.Sim.candidates(cells)` returns compatible digits. `BU.Sim.compatible(board,component,spare)` returns bool. `BU.Sim.board(state,id)` / `component(board,id)` lookup. `BU.Sim.snapshot(state)` JSON clone; `restore(snapshot)` validates version/schema. `BU.Sim.validateContent()` returns errors array. `validateTemplate(template)` returns errors array; `exportTemplate(board)` yields versioned JSON authoring object; `createScratch(template,seed)` validates and returns isolated state (modified). `BU.Sim.nextAction(state,boardId)` returns {type,...} ordinary operation for test/attract support only (not production auto-play).\n# Cooperative adapter contract\n\n`BU.Network(options)` owns networking only. Options callbacks: `onState(state)`, `onStatus(text)`, `onStart(state)`, `onEnd(reason)`, `onRoster(roster)` (all optional).\n\n- `await detectHub()` connects once, attaches handlers, automatically ready; returns true inside active Hub, false outside. Root calls only after all game modules/presentation loaded. No title gate in active Hub. Callback onStart happens after actual zero countdown. Root MUST continue `tick(dt)` regardless local pause when `online`.\n- `await host()` creates PeerJS Cloud room, returns displayed short `code`; remains lobby until `start(caseId, seed)`. `await join(code)` resolves after host acceptance/full snapshot; room errors reject with actionable message.\n- `start(caseId, seed)` host only, locks party workload, emits onStart everywhere. Root may invoke solo start separately; network not required for solo.\n- `command(cmd)` sends ordinary Sim command with local authoritative identity. Returns local result for authority, queued marker for clients. Adapter supplies id/revision/case; UI supplies op + board/target/spare/tool/etc per sim contract.\n- `tick(dt)` run every native update. Host advances by wall elapsed online, clients never simulate. Snapshots 5/s max, reliable. `requestSync()` requests latest state. `leave()` closes peers/handlers and clears holds/claims. Hub leave UI disabled.\n- Public fields: `mode` ('solo','hub','host','guest'), `online`, `authority`, `seat` (null on Hub server), `state`, `status`, `code`, `roster` [{id,name,color,connected}], `running`, `ended`.\n- Hub gameplay cheats must be disabled (`mode==='hub'`). Standalone tuning host only through command or broadcast method `tune(patch)`.\n- `canPlay` is true only for a connected seated local participant while round running and not ended. Root must block gameplay for null seat.\n- `dispose()` calls leave; destroys only Peer created by this instance. PeerJS dependency vendored and loaded dynamically only in standalone host/join flow (or bundled source available as BU.loadPeer).\n\nHub scripts: vendor/hub/session.js must be first inline script, byte-for-byte unmodified. Tags: protocol 1 / shared-world / 1-4 / session-minutes 4. Standard Hub card handles readiness. Main should show brief standby text until onStart.\nclass Shell {\n  /**\n   * new E.Shell(game, { theme, screens: { name: spec }, settings: { items, onChange(settings, key) }, store: 'shell-settings',\n   * meters: .125, standard: true }): an HTML interface over the game (see the section's header)\n   * - theme: CSS variables (ink, paper, accent, muted, danger, line, font: a family, titleFont), and scale: the whole\n   *   interface that many times (a number, or a function of the window) for a TV across a room; tv: true picks one by\n   *   the window's width (14 px text at 875 px, up to 30 at 1,875)\n   * - screens: specs ui.show(name) starts from (the pause screen follows game.paused; screens: { pause: false } leaves it out)\n   * - settings: settings.items: the game's own settings, { key, label, type: 'toggle' | 'slider' | 'select', min, max,\n   *   step, options, value } (kept in game.settings, beside the standard ones)\n   * - store, meters: store: where an older version saved them; meters: meters per world unit for distances\n   * - standard: false leaves out the standard menu (the pause screen's Settings, Controls, Full screen and Quit, and the\n   *   settings' groups)\n   */\n  constructor(game, o = {}) {\n    if (game.shell && game.shell._sys && !o.system) game.shell.dispose();   // the game's own shell takes over from the engine's\n    /** game: the game it is over; o: its options; root: its DOM element (null in a headless game) */\n    this._game = game; this._o = o; this.root = null; this._stack = []; this._keep = new Set(); this._held = new Set(); this._last = {};\n    this._sys = !!o.system; this._std = o.standard !== false && game.o.menu !== false;\n    const old = store.get(o.store || 'shell-settings', null) || {}, S = game.settings;   // (settings saved by an older version are kept)\n    for (const it of this._items()) if (!S.spec(it.key)) { if (S._raw[it.key] === undefined && old[it.key] !== undefined) S._raw[it.key] = old[it.key]; S.define(Object.assign({ group: 'Game' }, it)); }\n    for (const k of ['sound', 'volume', 'motion']) if (S._raw[k] === undefined && old[k] !== undefined && this._items().some(it => it.key === k)) S.set(k, old[k]);\n    if (o.settings && o.settings.onChange) S.onChange = k => o.settings.onChange(S, k);\n    game.shell = this;\n    if (!this._sys && this._pauseSpec()) game.pauseOverlay = false;\n    if (game.headless || typeof document === 'undefined') return;\n    // rebinding: the next key or mouse button goes to the action being bound (before the game's own listeners see it)\n    const signal = game._abort ? game._abort.signal : undefined;\n    addEventListener('keydown', e => { const c = this._cap; if (!c) return; e.preventDefault(); e.stopImmediatePropagation(); if (e.code === 'Escape') this._capEnd('Not changed.'); else if (c.device === 'keys' && !e.repeat) this._capBind(e.code); }, { capture: true, signal });\n    addEventListener('pointerdown', e => { const c = this._cap; if (!c || c.device !== 'keys' || e.pointerType !== 'mouse') return; e.preventDefault(); e.stopImmediatePropagation(); this._capBind('Mouse' + e.button); }, { capture: true, signal });\n    addEventListener('wheel', e => { const c = this._cap; if (!c || c.device !== 'keys' || !e.deltaY) return; e.preventDefault(); e.stopImmediatePropagation(); this._capBind(e.deltaY < 0 ? 'WheelUp' : 'WheelDown'); }, { capture: true, signal, passive: false });\n    if (!document.getElementById('esh-css')) { const st = document.createElement('style'); st.id = 'esh-css'; st.textContent = SHELL_CSS; document.head.appendChild(st); }\n    const r = this.root = this._el('div', 'esh' + (game._corner ? ' esh-cornered' : '')), th = Object.assign({}, SHELL_THEME, o.theme);   // (the top-right column starts below the corner's buttons)\n    for (const k in th) r.style.setProperty('--' + (k === 'titleFont' ? 'title' : k), th[k]);\n    if (th.font && /\\d|\\b(bold|italic|normal|oblique)\\b/.test(th.font)) warn('shell:font', 'E.Shell theme.font is a family (\"Georgia, serif\"), not a CSS shorthand (\"' + th.font + '\"): the size comes from theme.scale');\n    const K = th.scale !== undefined ? th.scale : o.tv ? () => clamp(innerWidth * .016, 18, 30) / 14 : 1;   // (for ten feet: the whole interface that many times, still filling the window)\n    if (K !== 1) { const fit = () => { const k = Math.max(.25, +(typeof K === 'function' ? K(window) : K) || 1); Object.assign(r.style, { transform: 'scale(' + k + ')', transformOrigin: '0 0', inset: 'auto', left: '0', top: '0', width: 100 / k + 'vw', height: 100 / k + 'vh' }); }; fit(); addEventListener('resize', fit, { signal }); }\n    const host = document.body; host.appendChild(r);\n    const E2 = (cls, parent = r, tag = 'div') => { const e = this._el(tag, cls); e.hidden = true; parent.appendChild(e); return e; };\n    const col = this._el('div', 'esh-col'); r.appendChild(col);\n    /** the HUD's elements, by piece (hb, obj, vit, toast, tut, pr, tip, place, mini, keys) and the screen layer (scr) */\n    this._els = { hb: E2('esh-hb', col), obj: E2('esh-obj esh-pan'), vit: E2('esh-vit esh-pan', col), place: E2('esh-place', col), tip: E2('esh-tip esh-pan'), pr: E2('esh-pr esh-pan'), keys: E2('esh-keys'),\n      tut: E2('esh-tut esh-pan'), mini: E2('esh-mini', r, 'canvas'), toast: E2('esh-toast esh-pan'), scr: this._el('div', '') };\n    r.appendChild(this._els.scr); this._els.toast.hidden = false;\n    const mini = this._els.mini; mini.width = 176; mini.height = 158;\n    mini.onclick = e => { const m = this._miniLast, X = mini._xf; if (!m || !m.onClick) return; const rc = mini.getBoundingClientRect(), u = (e.clientX - rc.left) * mini.width / (rc.width || 1), v = (e.clientY - rc.top) * mini.height / (rc.height || 1); m.onClick(X ? (u - X.ox) / X.k : 0, X ? (v - X.oy) / X.k : 0); };   // (the world point clicked: a strategy game's camera goes there)\n  }\n  /** the name of the open screen ('pause', 'settings', 'controls', ...), or null while the game plays */\n  get screen() { const t = this._stack[this._stack.length - 1]; return t ? t.name : null; }\n  /** the player's settings: game.settings (the standard ones and the game's own, by key) */\n  get settings() { return this._game.settings; }\n  /** the pause screen's spec ({} for the standard one), or null without one */\n  _pauseSpec() { const sc = this._o.screens; return sc && 'pause' in sc ? sc.pause || null : this._std ? {} : null; }\n  /** does this shell give the standard menu (the corner's menu button and game.menu() open it)? */\n  _menu() { return this._std; }\n  /** open a screen: 'title' | 'pause' | 'settings' | 'help' | 'results' | 'map' or a name of your own, from spec over\n   *  o.screens[name]. A spec: { kicker, title, text (strings or functions), actions: [[label, fn] or { label, fn, act,\n   *  primary, disabled }], back (fn for cancel), rows: ['glyphs', 'invertY', '#A heading'] (settings' rows, changed as on\n   *  the settings screen) }. From another screen it stacks (back() returns); shown again, it updates\n   *  (the new spec over the old). 'settings' takes its items\n   *  - 'title': { name, subtitle, meta: [...], footer }\n   *  - 'help': { keys: [[key, what]], sections: [[heading, text]] }\n   *  - 'results': { emblem, stats: [[value, label]], medals: [[label, earned]] }\n   *  - 'map': { map, at, markers (a list or a function), labels: [[text, x, y]], onPick(x, y) } */\n  show(name, spec = {}) {\n    const top = this._stack[this._stack.length - 1], again = top && top.name === name, s = Object.assign({}, this._o.screens && this._o.screens[name], again ? top.s : null, spec);\n    if (again) this._stack.pop();   // the same screen again: updated in place\n    else { if (top) top.focus = this._focusAt(); this._status = ''; }   // (the focus comes back here when the new screen closes)\n    this._stack.push({ name, s, focus: again ? top.focus : undefined }); this._capEnd(); this._render(); return this;\n  }\n  /** what cancel does: the screen's own back(), or close it (back to the screen under it; closing the pause screen resumes) */\n  back() {\n    const top = this._stack[this._stack.length - 1]; if (!top) return this;\n    if (top.s.back) { top.s.back(); return this; }\n    this._stack.pop(); this._capEnd(); if (top.name === 'pause') this._game.resume();\n    this._closed(); this._render(); return this;\n  }\n  /** close every screen (and resume, when the menu paused the game) */\n  hide() { const p = this._stack.some(t => t.name === 'pause'); this._stack.length = 0; this._capEnd(); if (p) this._game.resume(); this._closed(); this._render(); return this; }\n  /** the last screen closed: a game the menu paused (a title under its settings) goes on */\n  _closed() { if (!this._stack.length && this._ownPause) { this._ownPause = false; this._game.resume(); } }\n  /** which focusable element of the open screen has the focus (its index), or -1 */\n  _focusAt() { if (!this.root) return -1; const all = this._focusables(); return all.indexOf(document.activeElement); }\n  _focusables() { return [...this._els.scr.querySelectorAll('.esh-sh button:not(:disabled), .esh-sh input:not(:disabled)')]; }\n  /** close the menu: every screen over the title (a game's own title screen stays), resuming a game the menu paused */\n  _close() { const i = this._stack.findIndex(t => t.name === 'title'); if (i < 0) return this.hide(); this._stack.length = i + 1; this._capEnd(); this._render(); return this; }\n  /** a close or back button over the screen (an icon, 44 px; the keys have Escape and Start, so it takes no focus) */\n  _navBtn(cls, label, d, fn) {\n    const b = this._el('button', 'esh-nav ' + cls); b.type = 'button'; b.tabIndex = -1; b.setAttribute('aria-label', label); b.title = label; b.innerHTML = cornerIcon(d);\n    b.onclick = e => { e.stopPropagation(); fn(); }; return b;\n  }\n  /** a sideways swipe on a screen goes back: the panel follows the finger and goes back past 70 px (a slider keeps its own) */\n  _swipe(sh, box, title) {\n    if (title) return; let s0 = null, swiped = false;\n    const end = e => { if (!s0 || e.pointerId !== s0.id) return; const dx = e.clientX - s0.x, go = s0.on && Math.abs(dx) > 70 && e.type === 'pointerup'; swiped = s0.on; s0 = null;\n      box.classList.add('esh-sw'); box.style.transform = go ? 'translateX(' + Math.sign(dx) * 110 + '%)' : ''; box.style.opacity = go ? '0' : '';\n      if (go) setTimeout(() => this.back(), 120); };\n    sh.addEventListener('pointerdown', e => { swiped = false; if (e.pointerType === 'mouse' || s0 || (e.target.closest && e.target.closest('input'))) return; s0 = { id: e.pointerId, x: e.clientX, y: e.clientY, on: false }; box.classList.remove('esh-sw'); });\n    sh.addEventListener('click', e => { if (swiped) { e.stopPropagation(); e.preventDefault(); swiped = false; } }, true);   // (the button a swipe began on isn't pressed)\n    sh.addEventListener('pointermove', e => {\n      if (!s0 || e.pointerId !== s0.id) return; const dx = e.clientX - s0.x, dy = e.clientY - s0.y;\n      if (!s0.on && Math.abs(dx) > 14 && Math.abs(dx) > Math.abs(dy) * 1.3) s0.on = true;\n      if (s0.on) { box.style.transform = 'translateX(' + dx + 'px)'; box.style.opacity = String(clamp(1 - Math.abs(dx) / 300, .3, 1)); }\n    });\n    for (const t of ['pointerup', 'pointercancel']) sh.addEventListener(t, end);\n  }\n  /** a message that slides in at the top for a few seconds */\n  toast(title, text = '', seconds = 4) {\n    if (!this.root) return this;\n    const e = this._els.toast; e.replaceChildren(this._el('b', '', title)); if (text) e.appendChild(this._el('p', 'esh-m', text));\n    e.classList.add('on'); this._toastEnd = this._game.real + seconds; return this;\n2401:    this.shell = null;\n2433:    if (!this.headless) this._standard(signal);\n2547:    const sh = this.shell, s = this.scene, play = !!s && (!!s.pausable || this.sceneName === 'main');\n2556:  _standard(signal) {\n2563:    const fsChange = () => { this.input._refit(); if (this.shell && this.shell.screen) this.shell._render(); };   // (the player's choice is what they asked for: fullscreen.toggle() keeps it)\n2592:  _canBack() { const s = this.scene; return !!(this.shell && this.shell.screen) || !!s && (!!s.pausable || this.sceneName === 'main' || !!s.back); }\n2599:    const sh = this.shell, s = this.scene; if (sh && sh.screen) { sh.back(); return true; }\n2619:    const sh = this.shell, s = this.scene, I = this.input, fs = this.fullscreen, open = !!(sh && sh.screen);\n2829:    if (this.shell) this.shell.dispose();\n2938:    if (this.input.padLost) { this._autoPause('controller'); if (this.shell) this.shell.toast('Controller disconnected', 'Press a button on a controller to use it, or play on.'); else this.note('CONTR [... omitted end of long line]\n2939:    if (this.shell) this.shell._step();   // its screens move the focus with this step's input, and hold it back from the game\n2947:    const I = this.input, zr = !this.paused && !(this.shell && this.shell.screen) && !!this.zoomRange();\n2981:    if (F.keys !== false && !(this.shell && this.shell.screen) && !I.stickOn) { const m = I.move(), sp = (F.speed || 300) * dt; dx += m[0] * sp; dy += m[1] * sp; }   // (a finger pans by its drag, not [... omitted end of long line]\n3003:    if (this.paused && this.pauseOverlay && !(this.shell && this.shell._sys && this.shell.screen)) this.r.overlay(g => {\n3309:    const row = document.createElement('div'); row.textContent = t; if (!ok) row.className = 'no'; s.appendChild(row);\n3950:    const sh = this.game.shell; if (!sh || !sh._menu()) return false;\n4028:  this._needEl.style.display = this.shell && this.shell.screen ? 'none' : 'flex';   // (Settings open over it: hidden while it is)\n9430:    if (game.shell && game.shell._sys && !o.system) game.shell.dispose();   // the game's own shell takes over from the engine's\n9438:    game.shell = this;\n9439:    if (!this._sys && this._pauseSpec()) game.pauseOverlay = false;\n9449:    if (th.font && /\\d|\\b(bold|italic|normal|oblique)\\b/.test(th.font)) warn('shell:font', 'E.Shell theme.font is a family (\"Georgia, serif\"), not a CSS shorthand (\"' + th.font + '\"): the size comes f [... omitted end of long line]\n9467:  _pauseSpec() { const sc = this._o.screens; return sc && 'pause' in sc ? sc.pause || null : this._std ? {} : null; }\n9584:  dispose() { if (this.root) this.root.remove(); this.root = null; if (this._game.shell === this) this._game.shell = null; }\n9588:  _el(tag, cls = '', text) { const e = document.createElement(tag); if (cls) e.className = cls; if (text !== undefined && text !== null && text !== false) e.textContent = String(text); return e; }\n9731:    if (name === 'pause') this._pauseActs(acts);\n9751:  _pauseActs(acts) {\n9807:    const pause = this._pauseSpec() && (!this._sys || (g.pauseOverlay !== false && play));   // (the engine's own menu stays out of a game that draws its own pause)\n13518:  grid's cells); world.cells(): every cell { gx, gy, room, seen }; game.shell.hud({ minimap: world.shellMap() }) (full engine).\n13657:  /** the explored map for E.Shell's minimap: game.shell.hud({ minimap: world.shellMap() }) (a cell a square, the save\n// The POINT-AND-CLICK kit's game (an adventure of three rooms: a dock, a tavern, a lighthouse; four puzzles, the nine verbs or a verb coin, an inventory, a riddle contest), readable: the starter's shell (src/starter/00-shell.js), then\n// the slice from line 159 (src/starter/15-pointclick.js: its header says what it plays and how), then the start (90-start.js), as the\n// single-file kit holds them between GAME START and GAME END. pointclick.html plays it; node kits/test.mjs pointclick runs it headless.\n/* ============================== GAME START ==============================\n * MY-3D2DGE STARTER: a title menu and vertical slices, one per genre (a genre kit file holds one; the file with every\n * slice adds an animation lab). Each slice is a complete small game, written to be copied and changed: its own header\n * (below this shell) says what it plays, its keys and touch controls, its deep links and how it works.\n * FULL SCREEN, ANY SHAPE: res: 'ps1', expand: true. 320 x 240 is the least the screen shows; a wider or taller window\n * shows more world (r.W x r.H grows), so every HUD anchors to r.W and r.H, never to 320 and 240, and every level fills\n * what a wide screen or an upright phone shows. Prompts name the player's own keys: prompt('attack', 'Sword').\n * To make your own game: change the slice closest to your genre into yours, with its own title, play and game-over\n * scenes, and delete this menu and the slices you do not use; this shell (the game, its scenes, prompt, best and the\n * proof) stays unless you replace it. Deep links: #<slice> starts a slice, #<slice>/<part> jumps into a part.\n * ======================================================================== */\n(() => {\n'use strict';\nconst E = My3D2dge, { px, clamp, lerp, approach, TAU } = E;\n\n/* ---- SHELL: one game, several scenes. Each slice switches view, keys and resolution on entry ----\n * expand: the picture fills the window, any shape, with no bars: at least 320 x 240 (a slice's res), more on the long side */\nconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, view: 'side', bg: '#07060d' });\nconst P = game.particles, L = game.lights, A = game.audio;\nconst scenes = {};\nlet gpu = null;                               // WebGPU lighting, used by the adventure slice only\nconst best = (k, v) => { const old = E.store.get('best:' + k, 0); if (v > old) E.store.set('best:' + k, v); return Math.max(old, v); };\n// a prompt in the player's own controls, after rebinding: 'J Sword' on a keyboard, 'X Sword' on a pad, 'ATTACK Sword' (the\n// button's name) on a touch screen, where the buttons sit over the bottom corner: touch() says so, to move a prompt off it\nconst prompt = (a, word) => game.input.label(a) + ' ' + word, touch = () => game.input.lastDevice === 'touch';\n// each slice: its scene's id, its title entry, a line about it, and its family (the title groups them under it)\nconst SLICES = [\n  { id: 'adventure', label: 'ADVENTURE', about: 'TOP-DOWN ACTION ADVENTURE', family: 'ACTION ADVENTURE' },\n  { id: 'pointclick', label: 'POINT-AND-CLICK', about: 'RELIGHT THE LIGHTHOUSE ON GULL ROCK', family: 'ADVENTURE' },\n  { id: 'platformer', label: 'PLATFORMER', about: 'SIDE-SCROLLING PLATFORMER', family: 'PLATFORM' },\n  { id: 'metroidvania', label: 'METROIDVANIA', about: 'A WORLD OF ROOMS, ITS MAP, ITS GATES', family: 'PLATFORM' },\n  { id: 'brawler', label: 'BRAWLER', about: 'SIDE-SCROLLING BEAT-EM-UP', family: 'FIGHTING' },\n  { id: 'fighting', label: 'FIGHTING', about: 'ONE ON ONE, BEST OF THREE', family: 'FIGHTING' },\n  { id: 'shooter', label: 'SHOOTER', about: 'VERTICAL SHOOT-EM-UP', family: 'ARCADE' },\n  { id: 'arcade', label: 'ARCADE', about: 'A CABINET OF THREE CLASSICS', family: 'ARCADE' },\n  { id: 'pinball', label: 'PINBALL', about: 'ONE TABLE: RAMP, MULTIBALL, JACKPOT', family: 'ARCADE' },\n  { id: 'kart', label: 'KART', about: 'MODE 7 KARTS, THREE LAPS', family: 'RACING' },\n  { id: 'road', label: 'ROAD', about: 'A COAST ROAD AGAINST THE CLOCK', family: 'RACING' },\n  { id: 'puzzle', label: 'PUZZLE', about: 'FALLING BLOCKS AND FALLING PAIRS', family: 'PUZZLE' },\n  { id: 'rhythm', label: 'RHYTHM', about: 'STEP UP! FOUR LANES, TWO SONGS', family: 'MUSIC' },\n  { id: 'rpg', label: 'RPG', about: 'A JOURNEY: TOWN, WORLD, CAVE, BATTLES', family: 'RPG' },\n  { id: 'roguelike', label: 'ROGUELIKE', about: 'A CRYPT OF TEN GENERATED FLOORS', family: 'RPG' },\n  { id: 'tactics', label: 'TACTICS', about: 'TAKE THE HILL FORT, TURN BY TURN', family: 'STRATEGY' },\n  { id: 'rts', label: 'RTS', about: 'GATHER GOLD, RAISE AN ARMY, BURN THEIR CAMP', family: 'STRATEGY' },\n  { id: 'artillery', label: 'ARTILLERY', about: 'TWO TEAMS OF THREE ON AN ISLAND THAT BLOWS APART', family: 'STRATEGY' },\n  { id: 'sim', label: 'SIM', about: 'BUILD A THEME PARK, KEEP THEM SMILING', family: 'SIMULATION' },\n  { id: 'stealth', label: 'STEALTH', about: 'ROOFTOP STEALTH AND PARKOUR', family: 'ACTION ADVENTURE' },\n  { id: 'sports', label: 'SPORTS', about: 'TWO AGAINST TWO IN THE CAGE', family: 'SPORTS' },\n  { id: 'animlab', label: 'ANIMATION LAB', about: 'EVERY MOVE, SKIN AND VIEW', family: 'TOOLS' }\n];\n/* the camera: each slice's scene says how the player may zoom it, camera: { zoom: [min, max] | false, start, tall }: the\n   engine zooms it with the mouse wheel, - and =, a pinch on a phone and Settings, Camera zoom (the player's own, kept, on\n   top of the slice's start: tall on a phone held upright). Keys every slice shares besides: V changes the camera view,\n   [ and ] turn it (camera.rotate), 0 resets it (the player's zoom too), G toggles GPU lighting, M mutes */\nconst camOpt = () => (game.scene && game.scene.camera) || {};\naddEventListener('keydown', e => {\n  if (e.repeat) return;\n  if (e.code === 'KeyV') { game.nextView(); game.note(game.view.label.toUpperCase() + ' VIEW'); }\n  else if (e.code === 'Digit0') { game.settings.set('zoom', 1); game.resetCamera(); game.note('CAMERA RESET'); }   // (resetCamera draws it at once)\n  else if ((e.code === 'BracketLeft' || e.code === 'BracketRight') && camOpt().rotate) { game.rotateView(e.code === 'BracketLeft' ? -45 : 45); game.note('TURN ' + game.yaw); }\n  else if (e.code === 'KeyM') A.mute();\n  else if (e.code === 'KeyG' && gpu) gpu.enabled = !gpu.enabled;\n});\n\n/* ---- TITLE: a lineup of HD heroes on a torch-lit castle ledge under a dusk sky. The ledge is wide enough for a wide\n * screen and sits on the screen's bottom edge whatever its height; the logo and menu hang from the top ---- */\nconst MID = 480, stage = new E.PlatformMap({ rows: ['S'.repeat(60), 'S'.repeat(60)], legend: { S: 1 },\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"agent_message","text":"The game is titled **Bomb Union**. The source manual confirms the repair-and-deduction loop, fixed shape-or-color replacement rules, spoken work orders, and recoverable mistakes. I’m keeping those mechanics and adding a municipal response crew across the three planned cases. The fetched engine is the same v0.89.1 revision cited in the plan.\n"}}
